window.harnessChunks["rows-eb07d6f617c9a67a.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T16:47:33+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/BubbleBuffer/BubblePaw-Core\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BubblePaw Core</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/BubbleBuffer/BubblePaw-Core/tree/2f98827beda9f2c988368c99ee9fa69120899dff\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2f98827beda9f2c988368c99ee9fa69120899dff\">2f98827 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform workspace tasks through durable model/tool sessions and reusable child work actors. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain task progress, tool errors, context limits, interrupted external effects and child questions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose next action, tool, child task or final answer from the current observations. - Decision owner: The model actor driven by the native Harness model/tool loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SQLite history/effects, ToolDispatcher, CodeMode/workspace tools, provider adapters, approvals, queued steering and explicit resume. - Closure path: Prepared model request → model-selected tools → durable tool results → subsequent model request; child completion can wake the parent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Own runtime.rs executes the authoritative tool loop. The API provider witnesses inference-only transport; external Codex/Claude adapters do not donate their inner control functions. - Evidence: crates/bubblepaw-core/src/runtime.rs; crates/bubblepaw-core/src/dispatch.rs; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/agents.rs; docs/providers.md; docs/child-sessions.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed. - Boundary reachability: The normal bubblepaw runner wires Harness, tools and model providers; API provider mode and registered child profiles reach the same first-party loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate shared-workspace write interference between autonomous workspace child sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two workspace-profile children otherwise inherit the same filesystem and can overwrite one another; descendants may accidentally use the wrong root workspace. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose isolated child workspaces for concurrent writers and use returned branch/workspace state when continuing them. - Decision owner: The parent model actor selects worktree:true through the shipped tools.agent interface; git and workspace resolvers enforce that choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-child git worktree/branch, nearest-isolated-ancestor workspace resolution, scope-bound file/exec tools, status receipts and safe retention/cleanup. - Closure path: Parent selects isolation → child runs against its effective checkout → spawn/status returns workspace/branch/worktree facts → parent continues/steers the corresponding session without sharing its write surface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The credited decision is the agent-selectable conflict-attenuation mode, not deterministic git or session plurality. It is available to the running parent without custom host coordination code. - Evidence: docs/child-worktrees.md; docs/child-sessions.md; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/agents.rs; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/worktrees.rs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Optional mode; default workspaces are shared, and isolation does not automatically merge conflicting results or prove semantic conflict resolution.. - Boundary reachability: Registered model-facing workspace profiles expose the worktree field; agents.rs creates the binding at admission and subsequent child tools resolve that same workspace.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate current commitments and compute across a parent task and its descendant work tree. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stale/running/failed/waiting children, finite shared tool allowance/deadline and unfinished commitments competing for that allowance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect descendant status, cancel or steer child commitments, and assign fresh per-child tool/deadline bounds for the next turn. - Decision owner: The parent model actor owns these current choices within host-imposed maximums. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Descendant-tree status with unread/results, stale-child reminders, child completion wake, inherited budget/deadline clamping and subtree cancellation cleanup. - Closure path: State/reminder → parent observes current descendant tree/result → parent cancels, redirects or issues a bounded next turn → child runtime applies the changed commitment/allowance and returns status/results. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Credit is bounded to the parent task organization: the actor sees and can change ongoing child work on behalf of that whole. Host quota waiting and concurrency limits merely enforce ceilings; they do not own the parent decision. - Evidence: docs/child-sessions.md; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/agents.rs; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/agenthandles.rs; crates/bubblepaw-core/src/runtime.rs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Spawn/decomposition alone is not the witness. Controls cannot enlarge an active turn by steering; fresh limits apply on later child turns and remain clamped by originating bounds. No separate parent-human mode is inferred from approval UI.. - Boundary reachability: The ordinary runner model tool set includes agentstatus, agentsend and cancel with the originating parent ownership checks; no custom supervisor is needed for these decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No complementary independent operational-audit loop is supplied in the normal workspace runner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The possible organizational disturbance associated with the inspected mechanisms. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive organizational right established in this supported mode. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner established; developer/operator constraints remain external decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Context-recall probes/judges, replay/chapter-bench commands, direct child transcript access, logs, structured-output validation and ordinary tool checks. - Closure path: No corresponding organizational return loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Probe scoring belongs to explicit replay/evaluation rather than a normal-session corrective controller. Generic research/child tools can be composed into review, but no specifically wired independent audit judgment plus corrective return is shipped for normal workspace operations. Source-level verification is adjacent. - Evidence: crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/probes.rs; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/main.rs; crates/bubblepaw-core/src/observation.rs; docs/verification.md; docs/child-sessions.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No outside-and-then organizational adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The possible organizational disturbance associated with the inspected mechanisms. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive organizational right established in this supported mode. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner established; developer/operator constraints remain external decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Summary/chapter decision models, compaction/recall, selectable model roles, live provider configuration and research children. - Closure path: No corresponding organizational return loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Context organization and provider reconfiguration preserve current operation. Research and recall mechanisms do not by themselves investigate prospective environmental change and return selected capability adaptations. Replay model comparisons are evaluation, not a deployed adaptation authority. - Evidence: docs/decision-providers.md; docs/live-config.md; docs/context-compaction.md; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/agents.rs; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/probes.rs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity/ultimate-policy arbitration loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The possible organizational disturbance associated with the inspected mechanisms. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive organizational right established in this supported mode. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner established; developer/operator constraints remain external decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Ask/Allow/Deny/Auto approvals, model-based bounded auto checks, profile narrowing, live config updates and inherited child authority. - Closure path: No corresponding organizational return loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The returned decisions concern ordinary tool permissions and execution constraints, not an identity/ultimate-policy matter at this recursion. No first-party escalation and authoritative arbitration/return path for that function is packaged. - Evidence: docs/approvals.md; docs/live-config.md; crates/bubblepaw-core/src/approvals/mod.rs; crates/bubblepaw-core/src/approvals/modes.rs; docs/child-sessions.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9ff383b7ec14a8c25bdb4ee0ad185837493720aa/assessments/bu/bubblepaw-core.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9ff383b7ec14a8c25bdb4ee0ad185837493720aa\">9ff383b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:34:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/NeoPanic/harness3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Harness³</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/NeoPanic/harness3/tree/cb6c8e57eb0adac3f2ec1f4da1a98e52e2967063\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision cb6c8e57eb0adac3f2ec1f4da1a98e52e2967063\">cb6c8e5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute bounded user-published work through model-selected tools, report results, and optionally decide to split tasks for peer execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variable tasks, tool results, failed actions and results from completed children. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an instantiated Agent chooses next model/tool action, whether to use the board claim/split tools, and its work contribution. - Decision owner: model-driven independent Agent within the shipped Harness run; platform validates tool and task authority but does not choose the Agent&#39;s tool sequence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Preset bindings, runtime policy, receipts/checkpoints, SQLite Run state, tool-safety limits and claim prerequisite. - Closure path: executed tool output feeds subsequent model reasoning; settled Run and child results feed board continuation or user-visible contribution. - Boundary reachability: worker runner instantiates the shipping Harness with each Preset; normal board and Goal requests reach this path, independently of demo/test code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model has substantive task execution/splitting discretion; deterministic runtime and human task publisher set only operating constraints. - Evidence: crates/harness3-core/src/engine.rs#L65-L185; crates/harness3-worker/src/runner.rs#L55-L135; crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/board/mod.rs#L277-L325. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: high for source path; no provider-backed E2E performed. - Caveats: external tools may be unavailable and effects require idempotency beyond checkpointing; Demo output is not real research evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: resolve contention for the same operational work item and stabilize parent-child availability as peers execute and split work. - Distinct S1 units: separately provisioned model/tool Agent instances, each bound to a Run, Preset snapshot and independent local state. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing peers could both treat the same pending task as theirs; child and parent attempts could race while a predecessor is unresolved, causing duplicate work or invalid integration. - Attenuating coordination relation: atomic claim checks current task state and Preset compatibility, rejects a second claimant; staged children only become runnable after a successful source Run, while parent waits until all children finish; failed children block the parent. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a rejected claim returns a conflict to the Agent, pending/claimed state changes what may be read/claimed next, and completed children reoffer the parent for a new integrating Agent. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the specific multiple-owner race and dependent-work collision are regulated through conditional transactional ownership and state transitions, not just message delivery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: peer contention and out-of-order execution of shared task dependencies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the first-party board contract exposes task claim/split/feedback to peers, but collision arbitration is deterministic; no autonomous regulator decides among competing peer priorities. - Decision owner: deterministic SQLite transaction for conflict arbitration; individual S1 Agents independently choose which compatible task to attempt. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared board, generation/Run identity checks, atomic UPDATE, dependency state, event log and bounded task count. - Closure path: successful claim gives one execution owner; unsuccessful claimant receives 409 and can choose differently; parent is made pending after children complete or blocked on failure. - Boundary reachability: board tools are automatically injected into regular task-board Agent runs, not a theoretical adapter; claim enforcement occurs before work tools are usable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: coordination function and feedback path are real, but an autonomous S2 decision actor with cross-peer conflict discretion is not provided. The S2-specific constructor/claim primitive warrants C, not A. - Evidence: crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/board/mod.rs#L277-L325; crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/board/mod.rs#L333-L383; crates/harness3-worker/src/runner.rs#L18-L47; docs/task-board.zh-CN.md#L1-L39. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this is narrow coordination of task ownership/dependencies, not general negotiation of conflicting goals, cost allocation, global fairness or distributed consensus.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current operating capability/instance allocation against admitted work and shared resource limits, with distinct autonomous routing and human fleet-control modes. - Whole-system current view: pending board tasks, executable Preset catalog and model availability, active worker slots / reserved instances; in human fleet mode, reported capacity, fleet status and outstanding operations. - Current-control decision scope: choose which registered capabilities to provision for currently pending work; in parent mode change desired instance counts, scale or retire fleets under the current global quota. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uneven present task demand, incompatible/missing Presets, model availability, instance/worker scarcity and fleet size drift. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model router selects a Preset name/version/reason from the live eligible catalog for each actionable task; separately an authorized human chooses fleet target/termination. - Decision owner: autonomous bounded routing model in the base mode; legitimate operator in the distinct fleet mode. Deterministic Rust/SQLite performs candidate validation, quota, retry and reconciliation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: catalog filter, route timeout/backoff, global worker/instance limits, fleet revision checks, operation receipts, reconcile loop. - Closure path: accepted router selection is provisioned into a real instance and Run; failed/no-capacity decisions defer task for another attempt. In parent mode operator target writes desired state and fleet reconciler creates/drains actual instances, with operation/status returned. - Boundary reachability: model-backed router is wired into normal worker loop when a real provider is configured; fleet scale/terminate commands and the reconciliation loop are standard local API/worker capabilities, not a roadmap. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the base model makes a nontrivial capability-selection decision within live constraints, whereas Rust only validates and provisions. Human fleet mode owns a different concrete current-resource choice; this does not make all enforcement an autonomous S3 policy. - Evidence: crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/routing.rs#L91-L235; crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/routing.rs#L264-L315; crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/board/mod.rs#L243-L275; crates/harness3-platform/src/control/fleets.rs#L76-L137; crates/harness3-platform/src/control/fleets.rs#L202-L270; crates/harness3-worker/src/scheduler.rs#L20-L60. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: medium-high for real-model routing and fleet closure; no paid-model E2E. - Caveats: the router is a deliberately bounded single-call classifier, not an unlimited supervisor; its discretion is narrow current Preset choice. Demo uses deterministic selection. The human mode manages fleets and does not grant a human co-ownership of the same automatic board route in one run.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently inspect frozen operational contributions against specified checks, produce an evidence-linked judgment and drive bounded repair/acceptance gating. - Claim being audited: Goal work artifacts and final contributions support the specified acceptance/evidence checks. - Ordinary reporting path: operational S1 Runs and their artifacts/Goal contributions, including model-generated completion claims. - Complementary access path: a freshly allocated verification instance receives a frozen artifact manifest and separate paginated reader that checks hashes, read coverage and version; it does not inherit worker tools or writable workspace. - Independence boundary: new instance/session and reviewer-only evidence/report tools; different scoped role from original worker, even though both might use an LLM provider and the same operator config. - Who acts on findings: platform deterministically applies passed/failed/unknown/stale verdict states and, if preauthorized, launches bounded affected-task rework; human remains final Goal acceptor. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported claims, stale shared context, missing/changed artifact evidence, quality defects and false success on incomplete Runs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent model reviewer judges each required check as passed/failed/unknown with reasons and evidence references, separate from the originating worker. - Decision owner: fresh autonomous reviewer Agent owns the audit judgment; deterministic coverage/immutability gates and rework rules enforce/act on that verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: frozen hashes, per-artifact complete-read records, no plugin inheritance, bounded review timeout, manifest revision, immutable verdict, automatic rework quota and human acceptance. - Closure path: accepted verdict after successful reviewer Run becomes passed/changes-requested/inconclusive; failed findings can rerun related tasks and re-verify, while passed status gates later human acceptance. Stale evidence blocks acceptance. - Boundary reachability: optional verifier policy is supported on normal Goal and research workflows, and the worker runner launches its real configured Preset/model with restricted reviewer tools; it is not merely CI or Demo. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: semantic audit judgment comes from an independent Agent, not from the source worker or deterministic parser. Enforcement of complete reads does not magically prove external truth. - Evidence: crates/harness3-platform/src/collaboration/verification/mod.rs#L120-L263; crates/harness3-platform/src/collaboration/verification/mod.rs#L343-L616; crates/harness3-worker/src/runner.rs#L95-L135; docs/verification.zh-CN.md#L1-L42. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: high for shipped independent channel; medium for actual review quality without E2E. - Caveats: checks are chosen by the user; reads demonstrate manifest coverage and integrity, not independent verification of external facts. The shared task board alone does not activate this Goal verifier.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party externally sensing prospective adaptation decision loop changing the platform&#39;s future capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: prospective shifts in task mix, technology, model efficacy or external environment not shown as managed future threats/opportunities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no agent-owned search/option/adoption loop with external future signal and return into Preset repertoire or system strategy. - Decision owner: not established; operator can publish new Presets, and individual S1s can do user-requested research. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user-directed research template, Preset versioning, task router, shared context, verification/rework and plugin catalogue. - Closure path: existing Preset selection and repair react to present work; no demonstrated future distinction yields adopted capability change through an S4 route. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning, multi-agent research and selecting among already available Presets are not automatically S4. - Evidence: crates/harness3-platform/src/collaboration/research/mod.rs#L1-L130; crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/routing.rs#L91-L177; docs/managed-agents.zh-CN.md#L1-L115. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an operator could build an adaptive Preset pipeline but no in-boundary autonomous or parent-governed S4 closure is evidenced. ### Absence scope\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime ultimate identity/constitutional arbitration loop beyond static, operator-authored Preset and platform policies. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potentially conflicting organizational values or who the collective is; no first-party escalation of such a dispute is traced. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no exposed agent or legitimate parent path from a raised identity-policy issue through authoritative judgment back into next operation. - Decision owner: operator authors immutable Presets and config; these are static parent-set constraints rather than an evidenced ongoing S5 loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: versioned Presets, tool binding/security checks, human Goal acceptance, model router restrictions and cancellation. - Closure path: ordinary task approval/review and preset publication changes normal capabilities, not established policy conflict resolution after a surfaced issue. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: authorizing a tool, passing an acceptance check or selecting a task specialist does not decide constitutional identity or ultimate policy. - Evidence: crates/harness3-core/src/policy.rs#L1-L22; crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/routing.rs#L137-L177; crates/harness3-platform/src/collaboration/coordination.rs#L135-L175; docs/managed-agents.zh-CN.md#L1-L115. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the human operator does set top-level deployment constraints, but no function-specific proposed/contested ultimate-policy closure is demonstrated merely by being able to configure them. ### Absence scope\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2d0bcd786318bfe5e21d11d3c07a020adb044bba/assessments/ne/neopanic-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2d0bcd786318bfe5e21d11d3c07a020adb044bba\">2d0bcd7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-20T19:48:37+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/myagentroam/mar\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MAR</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-20\">2026-11-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/myagentroam/mar/tree/a4bd266e50a662f12e939fea43121912e39e0780\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a4bd266e50a662f12e939fea43121912e39e0780\">a4bd266 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform model-directed workspace coding and investigation in persistent sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Workspace state, tool outcomes and evidence change the feasible next actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the next tool or response and revise the operation after observed results. - Decision owner: The native session model, or the child model for its independent run. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Node capabilities, SDK tool loop, execution policy, state persistence and workspace authorization. - Closure path: Tool observations feed later model calls and the durable session result can enter its parent&#39;s context. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses and revises operational actions. Approval rules and the owner supply constraints. - Boundary reachability: Node session runtime creates the native MAR Agent and wires Workspace operations and the optional Agent Network extension in a regular Workspace session. - Evidence: Operational execution · Product session binding · Operator session usage - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Partition overlapping work between separately acting native sessions in a shared Workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel write tasks can target the same paths, and parent and children can duplicate research or implementation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a separable objective and read-only or non-overlapping write scope; use received child evidence to adjust subsequent work. - Decision owner: The coordinating native model in the Agent Network mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool-specific coordination instructions, scoped handoffs, parent-linked sessions and automatic status/result return. - Closure path: The child acts from the scoped handoff. Its terminal evidence returns to the parent model, which can change its own next work or send revised guidance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The coordinator model selects when and how to partition work within the existing Workspace rights. The runtime delivers and observes the decision. - Boundary reachability: The Node attaches the native extension with an explicit non-overlap coordination instruction and the supported child and feedback tools. - Distinct S1 units: Independent persistent native sessions can each handle a real deliverable with their own execution and history. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent agents sharing workspace files can overwrite work or duplicate an independently assigned task. - Attenuating coordination relation: The coordinating model hands off separable tasks with a read-only or disjoint write scope, and the parent is instructed to avoid duplicate work while children run. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Child output and terminal observations are returned into the parent context before the next model request, allowing a changed task or follow-up. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The installed extension specifically directs regulated allocation of shared Workspace write scope to reduce overlapping changes, and wires observation of the independent work back to the coordinator. - Evidence: Scoped coordination and tool binding · Child-result observation · Collaboration and shared-workspace warning - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Scopes in task text rely on model behavior. No dedicated per-child filesystem reservation or isolation is claimed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Inspect and revise authorized current commitments across a Workspace session portfolio. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A current Goal can become unnecessary, compete with other work, or need an intervention as session evidence changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select the target commitment to stop, steer, or revise using visible current state. - Decision owner: The coordinating model in Agent Network mode, or the actual run owner in the distinct direct workbench mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bounded session list/view, status and Goal projection, ownership checks, target authorization and run-control machinery. - Closure path: The selected stop clears its Goal and interrupts the execution. A revised Goal or replacement input changes subsequent work, and returns an operation result. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model or human chooses the affected commitment. Authorization and run control enforce the decision but lack the discretion to choose it. - Boundary reachability: The native Agent Network exposes actual list/view and stop/Goal operations across authorized sessions. The workbench exposes the same bounded workspace to its owner through session list/tree and run controls. - Whole-system current view: The portfolio of visible authorized sessions in the chosen Node and Workspace can be listed and refined with state, goals and activity for each session. - Current-control decision scope: Agent intervention can stop an authorized target or replace its durable Goal. The workbench run owner can independently select and stop or steer current work from the workspace session surfaces. - Evidence: Authorized current view · Stop and Goal intervention · Owner intervention from session list/tree · Workbench run control - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Whole-current scope is the authorized Workspace portfolio at one Node. This claim does not extend to other workspaces, users&#39; nondelegated runs or the installation&#39;s enterprise estate.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Enable a complementary examination of implementation claims and return findings to ongoing work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A session narrative can omit actual workspace changes or defects visible to a reviewer. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the claim to challenge, inspect raw workspace evidence and issue corrective findings. - Decision owner: Separately composed review session or human reviewer, with the coordinator or operator acting on its findings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Independent session histories and tool access, shared Workspace artifacts, Git/file inspection, review guidance and returned child outcomes. - Closure path: The reviewer can send its findings or end a child run with them; the coordinator can steer implementation after receiving the result. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A first-party constructor combines separate evidence access and a feedback path. The operator or coordinator must compose an adequately independent reviewer and meaningful trigger. - Boundary reachability: The documented native reviewer pattern can start a separate session with read-only task scope, use Workspace file or Git evidence, and use the same shipped Agent Network to return results. - Claim being audited: Whether the implementation agent&#39;s reported changes and output match the material Workspace artifacts and satisfy the review objective. - Ordinary reporting path: The implementation session reports in its own transcript and standard child outcome. - Complementary access path: A separate reviewer session can directly inspect files or Git changes. The operator can inspect patch resources or the net Git state independently of the agent&#39;s summary. - Independence boundary: Reviewer execution and history are separate from implementer reporting. The review must be composed and given direct artifact access rather than only a summary. - Who acts on findings: The coordinator can return findings to the implementation session through guidance or replacement work. The operator can also send a corrective message. - Evidence: Documented independent review pattern · Direct patch resource inspection · Independent Workspace evidence · Return and corrective intervention - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Patch resources cover individual applypatch operations. Git and files are needed for net changes and shell writes. The review role and adequate independence require explicit composition.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Introduce an externally observed model option into subsequent Workspace operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The configured provider can list new, removed or changed model options and associated limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select which observed live provider model to import, configure and use in later work. - Decision owner: Authorized model owner or administrator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Credential-backed live catalog, import form, settings persistence, authorization and runtime model resolution. - Closure path: Imported model records become candidates for authorized later sessions. The selected use reaches the Node&#39;s operating model configuration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The authorized human selects adoption using the externally observed option. Fetching and persisting it are deterministic. - Boundary reachability: Personal models and credential-backed catalog import are documented in the Community-capable model management surface and wired through Server Core and the Node runner. - External distinction: The credential-backed provider catalog is fetched live outside the Workspace. - Future / prospective distinction: Selected imports add a model capability to future native sessions independently of existing model entries. - Adaptation option generated: A returned provider model and its supported limits form an importable candidate. - Path back into current capability / S3: The persisted enabled model becomes available in authorized session model selection and affects future execution. - Evidence: Provider catalog sensing · Config management and import routes · User-operated adoption and model eligibility - Basis: structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Resolve ultimate policy and identity tensions in the assessed Workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Persistent instructions, workspace ownership and model eligibility can shape operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No Community Workspace identity-level decision path with an authoritative return is evidenced. - Decision owner: Operator controls Workspace instructions and approved execution mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persistent session prompt, Goal settings, run-control ownership and resource authorization. - Closure path: Next executions take revised prompts or access controls. These are session or resource-level changes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model exercises task-level and current-control discretion inside existing authority. The operator surfaces do not present an installed ultimate-policy resolution loop at this boundary. - Evidence: Persistent session settings · Run and queue controller ownership · Operating and model policy modes · Enterprise boundary - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c332c6541718ec4e71b3b4f4944818a81bee102d/assessments/ma/mar.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c332c6541718ec4e71b3b4f4944818a81bee102d\">c332c65 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-20T18:09:58+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/L1nkkkk/CrossAgents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CrossAgents</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-20\">2026-11-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/L1nkkkk/CrossAgents/tree/3699662a2bae328ef2226fb8229e7d2b3d1f388f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3699662a2bae328ef2226fb8229e7d2b3d1f388f\">3699662 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: plan-directed worker sessions carry out context-sensitive code edits, terminal/tool operations and task reporting in their own Git workspaces. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar code, changing task requirements, compilation failures, reviewer feedback and changing task outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a worker chooses executable edits and repairs, tools and completion/blocked responses within its assigned scope; the lead may also execute a solo task. - Decision owner: the configured native or ACP model-backed worker session under the Hub&#39;s source-native Work/Run contract. The external driver performs model execution; it does not supply the Hub&#39;s project task arrangement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runnable role sessions, scoped Hub tools and tokens, isolated Git worktree, task creation/assignment/report, session host and task state. - Closure path: worker returns changes and taskcomplete; Hub runs checks and returns failures to the same/new worker session with a concrete repair trace, with reviewer findings likewise fed back. - Boundary reachability: packaged crossagents run or GUI Work creates the lead and worker task arrangement; no custom adapter implementation needed for built-in native drivers, though an authenticated provider is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without model-backed task actors, the Hub enforces static state/acceptance but cannot make substantive code or task-action choices. - Evidence: worktree tasks; run creation and launch; first-party source README; runtime-host handoff. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: high for configured installed agent sessions; source-only assessment, not live execution. - Caveats: The model-backed worker requires a configured supported provider; the source-only review has not executed a live CLI session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference between distinct S1 coder/reviewer work cells competing for source paths, Git integration/base state and shared build resources. - Distinct S1 units: two or more separately assigned worker tasks with overlapping repository paths plus the reviewing/integration task chain. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent edits can clash in the same file, a worker can begin before a dependency is integrated, and concurrent builds can corrupt or block shared target directories. - Attenuating coordination relation: plan::scopesoverlap, readytasks and predecessor/stage restrictions delay conflicting workers even when more slots are available; the Hub materializes distinct worktrees and acquires checkout/resource locks; integration/landing requires task and check states to be current. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a task does not start until dependencies/stages/scopes are compatible; failed checks or resource conflicts become bounded notices/retry/repair paths rather than silent parallel writes. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: source readytasks tests overlapping scopes across simultaneously active S1 cells, with concrete negative tests; it is a disturbance-specific inter-cell exclusion, not just dispatch or an event stream. - Decisive decision or feedback right: gate conflicting work and revise runnable set against actual in-flight tasks. - Decision owner: source-encoded deterministic Hub scheduler, lock and Git state machine; user/lead may choose scopes and max parallel but no evidence that an independent model owns the general coordination policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dependency graph, scope matcher, separate worktrees, resource locks, task acceptance and Git integration. - Boundary reachability: built-in Hub/worktree path, not a custom extension. - Evidence: overlap and scheduling plus tests; resource-locked verification; Git workspace manager; dependency-aware task operations. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: C reflects a genuine first-party S2 control relation but no autonomous decision over dynamic coordination-policy redesign. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Contending worker file edits, stale dependency commits and shared verification resources can interfere across separate S1 cells. - Closure path: A pending task waits on overlap/merge/stage gates; once releases occur the scheduler re-admits that worker into its separate worktree. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The coordination path is first-party and interference-specific, but the Hub&#39;s deterministic Rust scheduler makes the scope-conflict decisions, not an autonomous coordinator.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: keep the current whole Work/Run coherent: build and adapt a task graph, select workers, handle check/review failures, arbitrate blocked tasks, decide next assignments and final integration readiness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing task demands, model/account limits, incomplete work, failed acceptance checks and occupied role sessions. - Whole-system current view: the lead holds the run&#39;s planning/tool context, task reports, dependency and reviewer outcomes, pending human cards and resume/continuation snapshots; the deterministic Hub supplies actual state. - Current-control decision scope: the lead&#39;s model-backed session submits/revises task plans, assigns worker/reviewer roles, makes task accept/rework/drop decisions and escalates/checks the result, not merely records worker messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the present allocation/intervention and next work after evidence comes back across work cells. - Decision owner: model-backed lead session; Hub only enforces the chosen plan, limits and checks. - Distinct parent mode: Approval in engine.rs explicitly accepts a human-selected RunPolicy, maxparallel and per-task assignees; a plan requiring human approval does not begin work until that owner decision is returned to the live Run. This is substantive whole-run allocation, not a generic shell approval. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: validated plan DAG, agent/session claims, status transitions, budget/quotas, continuation notices and current-check gating. - Closure path: task results/checks/reviewer decisions return to the lead, which submits new task/acceptance interventions; in parent mode the approved/modified task-owner and parallelism allocations become the Hub&#39;s executable plan. - Boundary reachability: shipped run approve and GUI human plan form; base mode uses packaged agent tools; model CLI account needed for actual decisions. - Evidence: run-wide Approval fields and run control; ready-task decision support; human plan and Work decisions; task decision tools; resume and escalation. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: human plan changes constitute the bounded parent S3 mode; individual tool approval does not.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge a completed code task using complementary actual Git diff and acceptance evidence before accepting/integrating it. - Claim being audited: a worker&#39;s implementation and task-complete report are fit for the declared task. - Ordinary reporting path: worker&#39;s taskcomplete, report and verification checks returned through Hub. - Complementary access path: optional reviewer is assigned a separate session with its own review round; receives the concrete diff and verified commit SHA (not only worker&#39;s narrative), submits structured reviewsubmit verdict with identified P0–P3 findings and which commit was reviewed. - Independence boundary: reviewer has its own session role and direct source/diff access, not the worker&#39;s arbitrary completion comment; the user may choose the same provider/model family, so semantic independence is structural, not statistically certified. - Who acts on findings: Hub verifies verdict/finding consistency (P0 blocks merge) and ties it to the reviewed SHA; a change request returns to the worker for a bounded next iteration, while a pass unlocks the lead&#39;s acceptance. Disputes go to lead intervention, not automatic suppression. - Decisive decision or feedback right: code-quality judgment based on complementary source evidence, distinct from ordinary done status. - Decision owner: separately configured model-backed reviewer in the ordinary shipped review mode; deterministic parser/checks alone do not create the substantive review judgment. - Closure path: afterverified directly opens a reviewer round when reviewertemplate or Run reviewer is configured; the Hub routes findings back and revalidates commit identity; standard CLI --reviewer and GUI route, no developer-authored plugin. - Evidence: review verdict/finding semantics and return; review tool, report and work return; reviewer role selection; verification evidence source. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high as available review mode; not asserted in reviewer-disabled runs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A task may be marked complete although its patch contains defects unseen by ordinary worker reporting or basic deterministic checks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Reviewer identity and Git reviewed SHA are persisted with structured findings; verdict freshness and rework routing are enforced by the Hub. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A distinct model-backed reviewer can make semantic judgments on the original Git diff; Hub SHA checks by themselves would be deterministic support. - Caveats: Optional reviewer sessions are required for this state; same-worker self-review, unconfigured mode and deterministically run tests alone do not qualify. - Boundary reachability: The installed run accepts a reviewer option and opens a separate review session after verified completion; no custom extension must be implemented.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: an in-boundary prospective intelligence controller that samples the external future environment, constructs strategic adaptation options and returns selected changes into present whole-system commitments. - Missing control path: the shipped expertise candidate/evaluation/acceptance workflow learns from completed internal task attempts and local lessons; the continuation watcher restarts current task progress; the browser and Web/ACP tools are available to ordinary S1 tasks, not a dedicated ongoing external-horizon future-option owner. No first-party S4 planning/adaptation episode or trigger found in the inspected shipped orchestration, expertise, policy and task code paths. - Missing decision owner: a human may accept/reject an expertise lesson and any agent may research in its assigned task, but no source-supported role has decisional authority to compare future external opportunities and choose portfolio adaptation for this local team. - Missing return/closure: saved internal expertise is recalled in subsequent coding contexts; it is not a forward-looking outside-signal proposal whose adoption alters the governing work/capability plan. Human accepting a lesson is not a future-adaptation transaction. - Evidence: internal-only candidate origin and human acceptance; stale/conflict maintenance; current-work continuation only; Hub task orchestration; policy surface. - Basis: bounded source absence, not assertion that future features/extensions are impossible. - Confidence: medium for this pinned standard distribution and local recursion. - Falsifier: a first-party, shipped external environmental-signal-to-prospective adaptation decision plus an executable return into the team&#39;s current commitments would change this state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future changes in external technology, project landscape and available capabilities would require a prospective organizational adaptation channel. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An owner would need to select an external-signal-informed future response and return a new current operating capability; this right is not established. - Decision owner: No first-party future-intelligence decision maker has been identified within the shipped local Work/Run mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Browser and expertise tools support present coding and internal retrospection, not a shipped prospective adaptation controller. - Closure path: No first-party external future signal → candidate option → endorsed capability change → present-S3 feedback chain was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: User-authored extensions could add an S4 actor, but the standard shipped system only supplies immediate task tools and retrospective experience. - Caveats: Bounded source absence, not a claim about all third-party extensions, future releases or external coding models.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: an ultimate identity/constitutional-policy authority for the installed local team, with a distinct policy-conflict trigger and returned decision binding the operating S3/S4 loops. - Missing control path: RunPolicy governs tool approvals, plan timing, budgets and landing; humanask resolves immediate execution questions; no source-native identity-policy dispute or S3–S4 tension instance is raised to an accountable final policy owner in the shipped Work/Run domain. - Missing decision owner: a user can set run policies and approve plans/landings, but those are concrete execution/work allocation decisions; the code does not establish a separate local-team ultimate purpose decision authority or constitution-review role. - Missing return/closure: permission and landing approvals are applied to an existing run and do not show a final identity-policy ruling returning to reconstitute the team&#39;s governing objective and its future/current control contracts. - Evidence: explicit run policy types and scope; human question/approval route; operational permission decisions; integration landing decisions; work creation/approval. - Basis: bounded source absence. - Confidence: medium; claims about an external user&#39;s organization are outside this local installed-system recursion. - Falsifier: an issue-triggered ultimate-policy decision with explicit binding return into current S3 and prospective S4 rather than another command/permission setting. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Long-term identity or ultimate-purpose conflicts across the local team would require binding higher-level policy resolution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Issue-triggered ultimate-policy authority returning a binding new governing direction is not present at this recursion. - Decision owner: The user controls plans, tools and permissions, but no distinct in-boundary ultimate-policy adjudicator/return loop is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RunPolicy and human approvals enforce immediate work-level constraints; they do not arbitrate constitutional identity. - Closure path: No local policy/identity dispute → ultimate-authority resolution → changed S3/S4 governing direction return is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Project run approvals and public OSS maintainer choices are different recursion/decision scopes, not the team&#39;s shipped S5 function. - Caveats: A user&#39;s external organization may have ultimate policy, but its authority is outside this standalone installed Work/Run review.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a3726d316daadbd9738cd4e4f60a1e4c7fcc2b08/assessments/cr/crossagents.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a3726d316daadbd9738cd4e4f60a1e4c7fcc2b08\">a3726d3 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T19:12:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Gavriel-lab/luma-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Luma Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Gavriel-lab/luma-harness/tree/24dbf80a75a4bce048739bd2f149c5d447569b77\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 24dbf80a75a4bce048739bd2f149c5d447569b77\">24dbf80 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform a scoped work ticket&#39;s substantive file edits, project commands, test checks and deliverable/report production under the approved human workspace and acceptance criteria. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous implementation details, variable source files, compilation/test failures, path-specific edits and unexpected allowed tool outputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide concrete code/text modifications, permitted read/write/edit/run tool sequence, retry behavior and honest report of tests, artifacts and remaining unknowns. - Decision owner: the registered first-party-managed Pi worker model in PiRunner and runPiBridge, backed by the configured member&#39;s external model API. Luma controls the invocation/permission boundary; the unwrapped external Pi SDK is not credited with unrelated higher functions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-member API Profile, host model forwarder, authorized ContextPacket, short-lived Broker token, validateControlledPath, restricted tools and project command runner, event ordering, heartbeat/attempt lineage and staged delivery. - Closure path: approved task/dispatch → Pi worker claims unique attempt and obtains current context → model chooses allowed changes/commands → workspace/test feedback → submitted artifact paths/checks/unknowns → rejection for repair or controlled acceptance. - Boundary reachability: apps/desktop/connectors/pi/worker.mjs, runner.mjs and controlled-tools.mjs are shipped local executor paths and connected to current Broker authority. docs/PI.md explicitly distinguishes real read/write/test from API-only chat. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model-driven execution loop, Broker/permissions can accept only submissions and enforce scope, not choose task-specific edits or error corrections. - Evidence: pinned docs/PI.md, apps/desktop/connectors/pi/worker.mjs, runner.mjs, controlled-tools.mjs, bridgeBroker.cjs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for embedded Pi mode and first-party wiring, bounded by controlled fixture rather than live model account E2E. - Caveats: Pi project processes run with the host OS user&#39;s privileges, not an OS sandbox; real provider entitlements and other CLI versions remain independently qualified.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct implemented lateral conflict attenuation between independent worker operations with returned peer-behavior feedback is sufficiently established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple workers and dependencies coexist, and a second attempt can be stale, but no demonstrated runtime resolution of two independent worker edits/claims against the same contested file/resource is specified. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Broker deduplicates requests, invalidates stale attempts, enforces one member&#39;s current job/identity and sequences task dependencies; these are vertical task custody and S3 management, not an independent S2 policy/right for cross-worker disturbances. - Decision owner: constructor-authored Broker identity/attempt/dispatch predicates, not an autonomous peer coordination role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: attempt/job/request IDs, stale-attempt rejection, registered member binding, dependency-based dispatch, stored task status, ContextPacket permissions and revision handling. - Closure path: duplicate or outdated report/claim → Broker returns stale/conflict error or blocks wrong-version promotion → legitimate current attempt proceeds; no specific peer-pair source-write collision and returned inter-S1 remedy was evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: keeping attempt state consistent is real but chiefly current-task custody; neither message routing nor task ordering alone is enough to claim S2 closure. - Distinct S1 units: separately registered Pi or CLI workers assigned to independently scoped tickets/tasks under one approved project. - Inter-S1 disturbance: possible shared-folder edit collisions or conflicting dependency assumptions; shipped sources do not demonstrate systematic detection and resolution between these distinct workers. - Attenuating coordination relation: bounded task/attempt ownership and current-context protection; useful guards, but not source-supported lateral worker interference management. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: stale attempts are refused; this affects replacement attempts, not established peer workers negotiating or avoiding a particular cross-S1 collision. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: no S2-specific evidence survives the distinction, so positive S2 is withheld. - Evidence: pinned docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, apps/desktop/electron/bridgeBroker.cjs, apps/desktop/connectors/pi/worker.mjs. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high for inspected standard work path. - Caveats: a custom executor or human-specified workspace partition could prevent collisions; those arrangements are not shown to be a standard Luma-owned S2 feedback mechanism.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: a model-driven coordinator controls current ticket decomposition, assignment, review/rework and work-state changes under a user-approved plan version. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing work dependencies, failed worker attempts, insufficient acceptance evidence, delayed or lost executor heartbeats, partial plan revisions and stale reports across current tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect authoritative workbench, propose concrete scoped tasks and owners, invoke create-ticket/update-plan, connect or dispatch approved work, reject an exact report with reasons, or internally accept after review; decide what a returned failure requires within authorized project scope. - Decision owner: configured first-party agentcoordinator model using Luma&#39;s bounded MCP capability, while only the human owner can approve plan versions or grant final project acceptance. The Broker&#39;s static gate conditions remain constructor C support. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: xiaowuworkbench MCP, current snapshot, project-plan version, assignee constraints, scheduleCoordinatorWork, Broker mutations, attempt lineage, review/correction records and originating-conversation handoff. - Closure path: owner request / current snapshot → coordinator model selects plan, tasks, workers and corrective steps → user plan approval if required → Broker dispatch/worker execution → reports, artifacts, failures and reviews return → coordinator adjusts current work or returns exact findings, then escalates final approval to user. - Boundary reachability: apps/desktop/connectors/coordinator.mjs is a shipped model-facing tool; docs/PI.md and docs/ARCHITECTURE.md describe configured coordinator with actual workbench access; Broker authorizes the same endpoints. examples/coordinator-prompt.md supplies a supported coordinator role. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: coordinator model selects substantive decomposition and response to results through first-party-authorized tools. Broker validation is not the sole control decider, but neither can the model waive human plan/final acceptance. - Whole-system current view: approved project/plan revision, registered workers, task dependencies and statuses, active job/attempts, review reports, artifacts/checks, cancellations, rework and conversation-return status. - Current-control decision scope: present project execution, worker assignment and evidence-driven return; not future company portfolio strategy or constitutional identity. - Evidence: pinned apps/desktop/connectors/coordinator.mjs, apps/desktop/electron/bridgeBroker.cjs, docs/PI.md, docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, examples/coordinator-prompt.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for configured model coordinator and shipped current-control path. - Caveats: an API Profile that merely chats without an enabled coordinator/worker connection is not this supported S3=A mode. User approval requirements remain hard gates; controlled test fixtures do not measure autonomous model performance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: separately from the implementing worker, a configured coordinator model challenges a submitted attempt against actual on-disk deliverables and acceptance checks, returning a concrete reject/repair verdict before internal acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: worker self-report that overstates completion, omitted/incorrect artifacts, missing test evidence and incomplete task acceptance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: call artifact on a submitted job/path, compare its contents and reported checks with task acceptance, and issue a binding reject-review with reason against exact attempt/report identifiers or internally accept if supported. - Decision owner: an independently invoked coordinator model/reviewer role (agentcoordinator), distinct from the registered Pi worker and from its output generation; Broker validates target identity and enforces review decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: coordinator workbench snapshot/artifact/reject-review/accept, read of approved artifact path via Broker, report/check/unknown metadata, exact attempt+report ID, preserved rejected evidence and repair/redispatch, final human acceptance. - Closure path: worker submits report and actual artifact → current ticket enters review → distinct coordinator accesses the submitted artifact itself and acceptance evidence → model challenges/accepts or rejects exact report with reasons → Broker returns rejected work for correction and keeps old evidence; eventual positive review still awaits human final completion. - Boundary reachability: the shipped coordinator MCP exposes artifact for authorized submitted text files, plus reject-review and accept; README/architecture/PI and examples/coordinator-prompt.md require the coordinator to examine actual artifacts and checks before acceptance. This is a conditional configured coordinator mode with actual tool use. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a different model-driven actor can inspect substantive file evidence and formulate case-specific negative findings; deterministic report-format checks or a purely summary-based verifier are not the source of the positive claim. - Claim being audited: a particular Pi/CLI worker&#39;s submitted deliverables meet task acceptance criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: implementing worker&#39;s summary, checks/unknowns and artifact list submitted via report. - Complementary access path: coordinator artifact reads up to 16 KB of the actual allowlisted text file inside the authorized workspace, rather than taking only the summary as proof; additional task/check context comes from authoritative snapshot. - Independence boundary: reviewer role and invocation are separate from worker, but it can use the same underlying model service and has only bounded artifact access; this is role/evidence-path independence, not guaranteed independent provider/human audit. - Who acts on findings: Broker checks exact current job/attempt/report and returns rejected task for repair, preserving prior evidence; accepted work advances to pending final human acceptance. - Evidence: pinned apps/desktop/connectors/coordinator.mjs, apps/desktop/electron/bridgeBroker.cjs (artifact, rejectReview, attachCoordinatorReviewer), examples/coordinator-prompt.md, docs/PI.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; auditor evidence access is supported but configuration/actual invocation dependent. - Caveats: the automatically spawned background coordinator review prompt currently provides report summary, check list and artifact names, not proof that it read the underlying files. Report-only automatic review by itself does not justify S3=A. This positive ownership applies only to the separately supported model coordinator mode that actually calls artifact; if that path is absent in a deployment the score must be narrowed. Synthetic tests do not prove empirical reviewer independence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no closed first-party future-capability or external-environment adaptation function beyond current request/plan handling was demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new owner goals, past conversation memory and proactive/diary features are contextual inputs or auxiliary product modes rather than supported anticipation of how Luma&#39;s organizational capability must change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: coordinator chooses present plan revisions within approved project scope, and humans can change member/API configuration; no prospectively accountable S4 actor updates the harness&#39;s long-term operating repertoire. - Decision owner: external user/maintainer for future mission and model/provider choices, with current coordinator for bounded work steps. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: saved history and optional private memory, activity schedules, revision logic, dynamic task creation, development roadmap/docs. - Closure path: new/changed task → current draft or amendment → user approval and execution; no first-party model of an outside future environment → capability proposal → authorized changed future organization state is demonstrated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model revising a project plan after errors is present-work S3, not itself a future-facing S4 adaptation mandate. - Evidence: pinned docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, docs/PI.md, apps/desktop/electron/bridgeBroker.cjs, README. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a human team using Luma could run an S4 practice with it, or optional memory plugins could learn; neither is borrowed into core first-party operating ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate identity/policy adjudication loop for the Luma work organization was found. - Disturbance / variety regulated: owner identity, member-role configuration, plan approval, final acceptance and workspace/API access are fixed or changed through human settings and constructor checks, not deliberated as constitutional organizational policy by an internal S5 actor. - Decisive decision or feedback right: only the human user may approve collaborative plans and finally accept the project; coordinator/model tokens are explicitly rejected for those endpoints. - Decision owner: external owner for defining identity/mission and final authorization; static Broker/host permission code for enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent agentowner versus agentcoordinator identities, authority mutation channel, explicit plan-version approval, final user-accept route, protected profiles/context, current grants and no chat-based privilege escalation. - Closure path: human project/permission decision → verified host/Broker state → bounded execution → final human acceptance; there is no ultimate identity/policy dispute internally adjudicated and returned as a new legitimate standing policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a review/plan gate and coordinator leadership are real S3 controls but not autonomous S5 ultimate-identity governance; even strongly enforced role identity is not a deliberative policy function. - Evidence: pinned docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, docs/PI.md, apps/desktop/electron/bridgeBroker.cjs, apps/desktop/connectors/coordinator.mjs. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: high for explicit user-gated policy boundary. - Caveats: a real owner organization may independently govern an OpenSiro-style policy plane outside Luma; this assessment does not credit it to the executable public harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1753063c228215c86965ea25c038cbda1571e154/assessments/lu/luma-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1753063c228215c86965ea25c038cbda1571e154\">1753063 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T13:46:33+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AtomUICommunity/TKCode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TKCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AtomUICommunity/TKCode/tree/620235e49fa0d7a1c7d80136a00414d9a398a102\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 620235e49fa0d7a1c7d80136a00414d9a398a102\">620235e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute source investigation, edits, commands and verification in response to a bounded delegated coding assignment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unexpected source structure, execution errors, incomplete specifications and changing worktree feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Built-in model selects tools, edits and next actions after seeing execution results. - Decision owner: Built-in Agent loop for default session and general-purpose worker loops in ACP. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool registry, workspace roots, file-state checks, JSONL session storage, permissions and model APIs. - Closure path: Agent calls tool; tool executes and returns feedback; model chooses its next coding action until it reports completion. - Boundary reachability: AgentHost creates built-in sessions and AgentCoordinator creates separate executable general-purpose subagent sessions; both run Agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool and action selection are runtime model decisions, not a fixed script. - Evidence: model/tool loop, AgentCoordinator.SpawnAsync, AgentTool.ExecuteAsync. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Upstream first-party source reviewed, without running the .NET desktop UI or external model.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate interference among coding workers operating on a shared codebase and interdependent change tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent edits to the same file and inconsistent dependent work across independently executing subagents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ACP coordinator model decides whether tasks can run simultaneously based on non-overlapping file ownership, or must be sequential; can send corrective messages on conflict. - Decision owner: Parent model in first-party ACP role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Shared workspace and task registry, subagent names, spawn/report/message tools; per-session file mutation queues alone are insufficient cross-agent arbitration. - Closure path: Parent selects file-scoped assignments and parallel/sequential dispatch; receives agent reports; on conflict or gaps sends follow-up and changes subsequent worker tasks. - Boundary reachability: SessionRole.Acp is a selectable built-in role; SessionOptions.Apply installs the role and AgentTool/AgentCoordinator support actual first-party worker execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: File-overlap judgments and conflict resolution are instructions to a coordinating model; not merely mechanical shared task state. - Distinct S1 units: Separately instantiated general-purpose worker model loops, each editing its assigned source files. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Overlapping file mutations and dependent modifications that can invalidate another worker result. - Attenuating coordination relation: ACP instructs parent to serialize overlap and parallelize independent file ownership, plus resolve post-report conflicts. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Worker completion reports and post-hoc sendmessage cause parent to revise subagent tasks or execution sequence. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The explicit file-overlap interference rule and corrective feedback restrict concurrent worker actions for coherence, rather than merely distributing work. - Evidence: ACP role steps 3–7, AgentTool concurrency and messaging, Coordinator reports and notifications. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Source proves the model-facing ACP protocol and wired tools, not that every run successfully detects every overlapping write.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Manage the engagement&#39;s current task commitments, work assignment, integration and corrective interventions across executing workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incomplete delegation, blocked dependencies, failed implementation, inconsistent outcomes and integration gaps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ACP coordinator designs current architecture, creates tasks, assigns files/workers, updates task commitments, reviews reports and dispatches corrective and final verification work. - Decision owner: First-party ACP coordinator model, with human user resolving scope-changing choices and approving tool calls as needed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Shared TaskList, taskcreate/taskupdate/tasklist, AgentCoordinator, worker reports and inherited permission gates. - Closure path: Parent sets engagement task plan and owners, receives worker outcomes, revises tasks/assignments, orders corrections and final verification before reporting completion. - Boundary reachability: Built-in ACP role restricts coordinator tools, shared tasks are wired via AgentHost, and workers inherit parent task list in AgentCoordinator.SpawnAsync. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model makes task prioritization and corrective resource/commitment decisions across the worker system rather than mechanically enforcing a budget or timeout. - Whole-system current view: Parent ACP model&#39;s task list, assigned file boundaries, architecture plan and returned agent reports. - Current-control decision scope: Task decomposition, agent assignment, dependency/parallel sequencing, rework and final integration assurance. - Evidence: ACP controlling instructions, task management API, shared work/task registration. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: S3 is established for the optional ACP coding engagement, not for a project-wide institution or ordinary one-agent session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No separately owned complementary audit channel challenges the parent engagement management independently. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Defects or omissions in worker outputs can trigger routine verification, but independence is not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The same coordinator may ask a subagent to run tests/review; no independent auditor owns a privileged S3-to-S3 challenge right. - Decision owner: ACP parent delegates verification and evaluates worker reports. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ACP step 7 verification delegation, test/command tools, AgentTool reports and task records. - Closure path: Verification reports return through the ordinary delegated-task channel into the same parent, not an independent complementary audit channel. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A verification subagent is a worker appointed and scoped by the same plan owner; this is normal S3 quality checking unless separate access/independent challenge and corrective accountability are shown. - Evidence: ACP review/verification instructions, subagent report delivery, task tools. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A human could arrange independent review outside the shown standard runtime; not credited here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No demonstrated prospective environmental/capability adaptation beyond executing the current coding assignment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unforeseen code and user requirements trigger local planning and replanning, not harness-wide future adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ACP model plans the present implementation and handles source contingencies; it does not own an external-future capability strategy. - Decision owner: Agent/ACP parent for current work; developer/operator for future TKCode capability changes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan role, askuser, task creation, model/runtime selection and user-configured roles. - Closure path: Task plans feed current implementation, not an external-capability proposal into a distinct S3 management decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A code implementation plan or selectable model is not an organizational future/environment intelligence right. - Evidence: Plan/ACP role, session roles/options, README. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Real product roadmapping is outside the scoped runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No autonomous or function-specific parent runtime identity/ultimate-policy governance is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User instructions, security permissions and safety rules affect tool execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human approves or refuses individual actions; built-in modes and permissions enforce preset limits, not legitimate policy-level adjudication. - Decision owner: User/maintainers for identity and policies; code restricts tool scope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Ask/autoApprove/fullAccess, Plan approvals, role filters, prompt limits and workspace permission checks. - Closure path: Tool approval gates an individual action or plan; no identity-level conflict moves through ultimate authority and returns as revised governing policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Operator approval and static permission modes do not establish an S5 policy decision/feedback organization at engagement recursion. - Evidence: session permission modes, session approval bridge, role filters. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Corporate or repository governance should not be inferred from runtime UI controls.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/fd4915af9d488ef8b7aa4fc98764a8d5406c8e5d/assessments/tk/tkcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit fd4915af9d488ef8b7aa4fc98764a8d5406c8e5d\">fd4915a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T23:56:39+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dohwi/pipaya\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pipaya</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dohwi/pipaya/tree/dd5cdfd560c1499301a1f2fe4c07f9173a12d53a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dd5cdfd560c1499301a1f2fe4c07f9173a12d53a\">dd5cdfd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Persistent model-backed workspace sessions perform coding/investigation tasks and can use disposable helpers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User requests, file/command results, provider faults, context pressure and delegated observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose workspace tools, follow-up actions and final answers; choose whether to delegate or create a persistent peer. - Decision owner: The configured session agent, composed by the first-party SessionManager around Pi Agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pi inference/loop dependency, first-party tools, SQLite transcript/context state, queues, compaction, retry and limits. - Closure path: Prompt → constructed Agent with owned tool definitions → selected calls → observations/persistence → further turns and final response. - Boundary reachability: ensureLive creates Agent with the session&#39;s actual model, history, prompt and first-party tools. The packaged server and desktop-bundled server wire this composition directly. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party product owns the concrete executable composition; Pi SDK is a dependency and does not donate uninstantiated higher functions. Without the session actor, server APIs and gates do not choose project work. - Evidence: [Session construction and helper execution][R1], [tools][R2], [server distribution][R3], [manifest][R4]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: No provider-backed E2E run or Pi-wide capability claim is made.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: The supplied peer protocol attenuates reciprocal reply/notification oscillation between independently running sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two peer sessions can repeatedly reply to one another or cause completion notifications to trigger further replies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The receiving agent decides whether a peer response is needed and whether to end that exchange; the sender chooses explicit reply/notification requests. - Decision owner: The participating session agents own semantic response necessity; runtime-authored notification and send bounds constrain the exchange. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: peerMessage marks other-session origin and says to reply only if needed; completion watches are consumed once, reports say no reply needed, and per-run send overflow returns stop/report feedback. - Closure path: Peer message or one-shot completion report → recipient agent sees explicit reply/termination semantics → chooses further peersend only when needed, or stops; cap refusal feeds back to the sender&#39;s next action. - Boundary reachability: Peer tools are automatically included in normal session tool plans unless the operator disables them. PeerService wraps actual delivered messages and sends report feedback into prompt/followup paths of ordinary agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The narrow credited discretion is agent choice over necessary replies under a first-party anti-oscillation protocol. The generic mailbox, layout groups and delegation are not themselves the S2 witness, and the counter is not an autonomous owner. - Evidence: [Peer templates, send counter and one-shot watchers][R5], [tool descriptions][R2], [always-reachable peer tool wiring][R1], [tool plan][R6]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This is bounded structural attenuation, not proof that arbitrary agent conversations terminate. The counter resets each run and cannot alone rule out cross-run ping-pong or shared-file collisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: A session agent can regulate current commitments across peer sessions, and the local human operator has a distinct supplied supervisory mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Peers are stuck, working on the wrong course, waiting on questions, or consuming the wrong model/active-session capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect all peer state, steer/abort work, create/close/resume sessions, withdraw queued work, change model/effort and answer blocked questions; in parent mode the operator makes those current-control choices through the server/client API. - Decision owner: Base mode: a model-backed session acting as peer supervisor. Parent mode: the local server operator using the administrator-authority client/API. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: peerlist/read/current watcher notifications, peersend steer/followup, peerabort/manage/create/answer, persisted session state and authenticated REST/WebSocket interfaces. - Closure path: Current peer view/report → agent or operator selects intervention → SessionManager applies queue/run/model/lifecycle/question changes → subsequent peer execution follows the changed current commitment. - Boundary reachability: Every normal session receives the first-party peer tools, wired to SessionManager host methods, unless disabled. The client/API exposes server-wide activity/session/status/usage and actual abort/message/patch/question endpoints for its operator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the supervisory actor leaves APIs, messages and limits but no discretionary selection of whom to stop, steer, resume or reconfigure. Parent control is evidenced separately from generic askuser approval. - Evidence: [Peer state/intervention methods][R5], [specific tool semantics][R2], [SessionManager host wiring][R1], [authenticated operator routes][R7], [server API contract][R3]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Authority is broad among sessions of one trusted server; this is neither a global organizational claim nor evidence that every run uses a supervisor.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No shipped audit-specific independent challenge-and-corrective-control loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A peer or helper may make an incorrect completion claim. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supplied audit role/verdict/return protocol decides that claim independently. - Decision owner: Ordinary producing sessions report their work. Helper role files and extension logic are user supplied. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: peerread can inspect ordinary transcript/tool snippets; workspace read/grep and generic helpers are available; event/context/read tracing preserve evidence. - Closure path: Peer/helper output returns as ordinary tool text or messages, without an audit-specific independently controlled adjudication and corrective path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A fresh helper context and raw file tools can be used to build a reviewer, but the shipped delegate interface itself is generic operational delegation. No bundled reviewer is credited from an external marketplace. - Evidence: [Helper definition/execution][R8], [session delegate][R1], [peer read][R5], [extension boundary][R9]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Users can create a material complementary audit application; it needs its own independent owner, evidence-access and corrective-return proof.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider configuration, new skills/extensions, changing instructions and available upstream releases. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party intelligence path develops future-relevant options and negotiates a return into current capability. - Decision owner: Users configure endpoints/extensions/skills and request updates; sessions perform requested present tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AGENTS.md/skill refresh, compaction, provider settings, extension APIs and release-tag updater. - Closure path: Settings apply next run; updater installs the latest release after operator request and running-session checks. No prospective environment model/adaptation-option decision loop is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A latest-tag inequality check and package restart are maintenance, not external/future adaptation judgment. General capability extension and compaction are not S4; no parent adaptation mode is inferred from update consent. - Evidence: [Source update policy][R10], [live context/skills][R11], [SessionManager][R1], [extension skill boundary][R9]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: The assessment does not claim that users cannot organize an adaptive workflow on this product.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity/ultimate-policy adjudication-and-return loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Session instructions, permissions, enabled skills/tools and broad server administrator control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No identity-level issue is routed to ultimate authority and returned as an authoritative system policy decision. - Decision owner: Users author prompts/settings; trusted extensions run with server privileges; agents can change bounded peer metadata/current work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Authentication token, frozen initial tool/base-prompt snapshot, live AGENTS/skill settings, extension approval and per-tool enforcement. - Closure path: Settings constrain subsequent calls, and askuser/peeranswer returns ordinary question answers. Those are not a supplied identity-policy adjudication protocol. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A field named purpose, a user answer and administrator control are not S5 without a function-specific ultimate-policy issue and governing return. - Evidence: [Context and instruction boundary][R11], [session controls][R1], [question lifecycle][R12], [operator API][R7], [peer metadata][R5]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: The S3 parent mode is not reused as S5 evidence; external extension policy does not enter the first-party default boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/af57467e7adc84c630a0ffc6bb9cfb46e586a39f/assessments/pi/pipaya.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit af57467e7adc84c630a0ffc6bb9cfb46e586a39f\">af57467 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T21:17:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rokartur/agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agent (rokartur)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rokartur/agent/tree/f02a473edd8a08368876e45a641f8ed8c2a4c85d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f02a473edd8a08368876e45a641f8ed8c2a4c85d\">f02a473 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: coding transformation by root and child agent loops - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown source, tool failures, changing files and delegated findings - Decisive decision or feedback right: select tool/action/arguments and revise the approach from results - Decision owner: root/child models within their assigned scopes - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, permissions, tool dispatcher, JSONL persistence, snapshots and channels - Closure path: model tool choice → execution result → next model input; child answer/follow-up → root continuation - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model leaves execution support but no discretionary solution. - Evidence: source/src/agent/mod.rs#L539-L670, source/src/agent/subagent.rs#L188-L215, source/src/agent/subagent.rs#L263-L323 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Pinned source establishes a supported path, not observed effectiveness or universal deployment. - Boundary reachability: Agent::runturns is called by supported CLI/server modes; child launch uses the current installed executable.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent stale writes from overwriting another operational writer&#39;s changes - Disturbance / variety regulated: root/parallel children can act on the same cwd after reading different file versions - Decisive decision or feedback right: reject stale anchored edits and return fresh-read feedback; broader coordination discretion still needs composition - Decision owner: first-party anchor controller enforces the rule; no separately assigned autonomous cross-writer coordination owner - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session-specific anchors, reread/sync before edit, model-visible error and common child cwd - Closure path: another writer changes file → stale anchor rejected → caller receives ESTALEANCHOR and must reread before a valid subsequent edit - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A concrete collision-specific path exists; deterministic rejection does not itself own autonomous coordination judgment. C credits this narrow supplied path, not the ability to program any coordinator. - Evidence: source/src/tools/hashline.rs#L308-L329, source/src/tools/hashline.rs#L654-L659, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L975-L1004, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L640-L666 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Pinned source establishes a supported path, not observed effectiveness or universal deployment. - Boundary reachability: Hashline-backed tools are installed for the coding agents and child launch defaults to the root cwd. - Distinct S1 units: root coding agent and parallel child coding agents. - Inter-S1 disturbance: stale read-modify-write on shared files after a different writer edits them. - Attenuating coordination relation: anchor ownership plus reread/sync rejects stale range rather than silently overwriting. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: caller receives explicit fresh-read instruction in tool result, then chooses its next action. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited path is version-sensitive writer-conflict rejection, not child spawning, work ordering or a mailbox.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: current cohesion and accountability of the bounded child task team - Disturbance / variety regulated: different child states, failed/superseded work and model/effort commitments - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect team roster/results, change active child scope, stop and resume commitments, select model/effort for replacement work - Decision owner: root model for its current team - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ListAgents, SendMessage, TaskStop, subagent state and follow-up queues; static concurrency cap is enforcement - Closure path: team status/result → root current judgment → steering/stop/resume/new scoped resource choice → changed child operations → later state/result - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This is narrower than global fleet governance and exceeds initial decomposition: a running child can be steered, stopped and resumed after accountability feedback. - Evidence: source/src/agent/mod.rs#L892-L1029, source/src/agent/subagent.rs#L92-L108, source/src/tools/tools.json, source/src/agent/swarm-prompt.md#L18-L35 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Task-team scope only. Generic human plan approval, session interruption and permission-mode edits are not credited as a separate parent S3 mode. - Boundary reachability: Root&#39;s shipped tool set exposes status and intervention; follow-ups enter its continuing run loop. No custom control-plane code is required. - Whole-system current view: root&#39;s roster of its child tasks/status plus returned answers, within the task-team recursion. - Current-control decision scope: ongoing team commitments, task scope, model/effort resources and intervention; not provider infrastructure or unrelated sessions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge delegated outcome claims with complementary primary-artifact access - Disturbance / variety regulated: a child answer can be mistaken despite appearing complete - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently open cited source or rerun the decisive command before relying on the child&#39;s claim - Decision owner: root agent as a decision holder distinct from the child producer - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in swarm guidance embedded in Agent tool description, root Read/Bash, returned child claim and root continuation - Closure path: child answer → root selects checks on depended-on claims → raw artifact/command evidence → root revises or withholds next work and can steer/resume the child - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The shipped instruction explicitly treats the answer as a claim and names an alternate evidence path; judgment resides with the root, not a deterministic hook. This does not rely on naming an arbitrary spawned child &#39;auditor&#39;. - Evidence: source/src/agent/swarm-prompt.md#L18-L27, source/src/tools/mod.rs#L38-L43, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L1022-L1029, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L892-L920 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Prompt-guided supported audit mode, not evidence that every run performs it. Auto permission reviewer and deep-research&#39;s mandatory verify phase are not used to establish complementary audit. - Boundary reachability: The default bundled swarm prompt is injected into the root&#39;s Agent tool description; root already has source/command tools and consumes child results in supported modes. - Claim being audited: child&#39;s completion/finding claims on which the root&#39;s next step depends. - Ordinary reporting path: child final answer or queued background follow-up. - Complementary access path: root independently reads cited path:line or reruns decisive command against workspace reality. - Independence boundary: root context/judgment is separate from the child production context; model provider may be shared, so this is workflow/claim independence, not model-diversity assurance. - Who acts on findings: root changes its own next step or sends corrective child instructions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: outside-and-then adaptation of the harness/team capability - Disturbance / variety regulated: Web research, long-term goals and self-update are not by themselves prospective system adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive path established. - Decision owner: None established for this function in the assessed mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bundled deep-research/workflows, goal/todo/cron, model routing, compaction, update and skills - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific return into operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deep-research produces the requested research report; updates follow published versions. No shipped loop turns external prospective distinctions into adaptation options and a current-capability regulatory return; parent adaptation was not established. - Evidence: source/src/agent/deep-research.js, source/src/update.rs, source/src/tools/goal.rs, source/src/agent/workflow.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: identity / ultimate-policy closure - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ask/Auto/FullAccess permissions, trust and prompt policy bound ordinary tool actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive path established. - Decision owner: None established for this function in the assessed mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission reviewer, trusted project instructions, plan approval, settings, root swarm constraints and session UI - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific return into operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The review judges an individual proposed action against the existing request; it neither resolves identity-policy tension nor constructs an authoritative policy-return loop. Mutable prompts and contributor governance do not close S5 for the deployed task team. - Evidence: source/src/agent/permissions.rs#L178-L229, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L1106-L1141, source/src/agent/prompt.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/28430b0a3ee80e366fca884d46873921a7deb469/assessments/ag/agent-rokartur.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 28430b0a3ee80e366fca884d46873921a7deb469\">28430b0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-18T18:46:20+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rohanarun/Auto-research-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Auto-research agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-18\">2026-11-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rohanarun/Auto-research-agent/tree/bdd59df709d0b5353856b1cc39e116226c57f5d4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision bdd59df709d0b5353856b1cc39e116226c57f5d4\">bdd59df &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-guided investigation and tool-using iteration on a research project&#39;s open problem. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown technical hypotheses, source code, experimental outcomes, external CLI results and changing evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose code/experiment actions, interpret tool feedback, update candidate hypotheses and return task results. - Decision owner: Configured model-driven coding/research worker in its first-party app-controlled session; provider inference is outside the repository. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Swift session runner, supported CLI, local or cloud checkout, task instructions, timers and saved transcripts. - Closure path: A worker reads current checkout/check outputs, makes further changes, saves evidence and returns results to the app/project and next run. - Boundary reachability: Standard Play launches installed configured Codex/Claude/OpenCode/Antigravity for a research project; the optional method runner launches the same supported CLI in branch workspaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model selects substantive experiments and edits; app control and the CLI transport do not decide those scientific actions. - Evidence: normal shipped execution, model-driven method invocation, branch execution and evaluated returns. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate branch-contention and duplicated incompatible research among multiple independent model-backed S1 workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two writers can contend for one checkout; separate agents can repeat already failed or overlapping experimental directions and waste the common time/compute allowance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In supported shared-context mode, each agent chooses a complementary experiment after considering peer checkpoint evidence; the coordinating model determines work partitions within user-approved limits. - Decision owner: Model workers and coordinating agent for selection of complementary research directions; runtime independently enforces checkout isolation and common resource bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Active-checkout guard, independent workspaces/branches, timed checkpoint bundle, immutable peer manifest, peer findings and rejection of malformed collaboration. - Closure path: An active writer blocks competing startup; after the shared checkpoint boundary, next agent sessions receive peer findings and choose changed experiments; failed peer obligations prevent candidate promotion and return diagnostic evidence. - Boundary reachability: Opt-in first-party parallel sharing and method co-operation are shipped Swift/UI modes, with native snapshot/restart and bundled method Python runner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Checkout locks are enforcement only; the policy explicitly instructs the model to respond to actual peer evidence by selecting a different, complementary next experiment rather than relying on static routing. - Distinct S1 units: Two or more independently invoked model research workers, each with a separate checkout and own experiments, whose results influence the same project objective. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent editing of a shared source checkout and overlapping/harmfully duplicated experiments competing for one fixed research budget. - Attenuating coordination relation: Project writer reservation plus private checkouts; peer evidence/checkpoints and model-authored complementary follow-up assignments. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The saved peer bundle is injected into the next branch session; model policy requires checking peers&#39; findings and choosing a distinct justified experiment, with collaboration failure stopping invalid promotion. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Concrete writer contention is detected at project launch; sharing is tied to avoiding redundant experiments and resolving peer evidence/ownership rather than merely passing a message. - Evidence: active checkout collision refusal, separate research checkouts and generations, peer policy and complementary experiment, method peer review and next cohort, shared cohort return. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Positive coordination depends on explicitly enabled multi-worker sharing/methods and model compliance with the shipped policy. Independent workers without sharing have only checkout isolation, not the full agent-owned S2 path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Maintain project-wide current research commitments, branch allocation, verified incumbent and finite resources as feedback changes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Branch exploration can duplicate work, run out of time/disk/provider capacity, or promote an unverified candidate while active workers still have useful unfinished results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In enabled methods mode the model coordinator authors the criterion-aware plan, experiment portfolio, per-branch metric views and whether more research is justified; in attended mode the owner can set stop/cost limits and intervene across project runs. - Decision owner: Model coordinator for the autonomous method mode; project owner for the independent parent-controlled project mode. The deterministic runner enforces ceilings and selects only evaluator-valid leaders. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Immutable plan and evaluator, parallel branch scheduler, score/metric dashboards, verified leader state, stop/drain/recovery, configurable method windows and cloud ownership ledgers. - Closure path: Runner returns verified branch/metric and outstanding-work evidence to coordinator; it chooses the next plan or stops. User Stop cancels project workers, while chosen budgets and sharing settings affect subsequent allocations. - Boundary reachability: First-party optional method policy invokes the configured coordinator model with project-wide evidence; app exposes per-project controls and returns archived results to the same authorized research project. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: In autonomous mode, model decisions about research allocation are distinct from immutable evaluator score ranking or time-slot enforcement. In parent mode, the owner can stop/limit the whole research cohort. - Whole-system current view: Coordinator sees DASHBOARD.json, evaluated leaders/branches, metric diagnostics and pending work; app shows project sessions and shared group status and can stop all owned workers. - Current-control decision scope: Select active experiment portfolio and whether to continue within an existing research objective, prioritize verified incumbent over speculative work, allocate branch count/windows within parent ceilings; parent can stop or change supported project settings. - Evidence: coordinator-owned plan and fixed limits, leader and branch control, continue based on returned evidence, owner group control, result/continued invocation. - Basis: structural -\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Complement production claims with separately executed source-artifact evaluation under a frozen checker, feeding accepted/rejected evidence into control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A writing worker can claim improvement without a correct or independently reproducible result. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Check true candidate validity/score and raw evidence against a task-specific evaluator prepared before experimentation and held outside agent-writeable candidate workspaces. - Decision owner: The separately executed evaluator makes the audit judgment; model/human establishes its scientific criterion before execution, while the first-party runner enforces checksum and independence boundaries. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Frozen evaluator hash, isolated subprocess, pre/post workspace inventory, evidence SHA-256, preflight valid/invalid fixtures and verified leader selection. - Closure path: Failed evaluator rejects candidate; a passing independent result determines eligible leader and returns a score/receipt to dashboard/coordinator before next research action. - Boundary reachability: Shipped optional ResearchMethods mode requires an external-to-candidate evaluator and runs it independently on source workspace bytes, with no user-added plugin protocol needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This is a first-party constructor path for a specific independent audit task, not a globally preinstalled independent researcher. The producer does not control the evaluator after it is frozen, but an operator/coordinator must supply a valid task-specific audit criterion and checker. - Claim being audited: Worker completion/improvement and score claims over its candidate source. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker output and its research checkpoint/transcript containing self-reported results. - Complementary access path: Outside-candidate frozen evaluator independently opens the actual candidate workspace, runs a task-specific check and hashes the cited evidence. - Independence boundary: Evaluator copied outside branch directories, its digest checked post-run and candidate inventory verified unchanged; evaluator cannot be edited by the candidate worker and cannot accept score without evidence. - Who acts on findings: Runner excludes failed candidate and chooses among evaluated valid leaders; model coordinator receives returned metrics and future-planning evidence. - Evidence: independent task-specific checker requirement, source artifact evaluation/digest/receipt, selection from independently valid candidates, rejected incomplete/invalid branches. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: A synthetic or dishonest evaluator is not an independent audit of scientific correctness. C is bounded to this implemented evaluator-construction protocol, not a claim of independent audit in every default task or a generalized S3 runtime.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No project-operating-capability adaptation loop grounded in external future/environmental distinctions was established apart from research execution itself. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Project topics, scientific unknowns, model/tool availability and research-method performance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model may choose future experiments within the research goal and runner changes branch strategy after a stalled score; no shipped agentically closed decision for adapting the research organization to external prospective conditions was shown. - Decision owner: Project model makes immediate research choices; operator edits project settings, model and available methods. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Research catalog discovery, configurable RSI-top/fork-and-flush, published metric dashboards and per-batch future tasks. - Closure path: Research method continuation feeds a new experiment into the same task objective; no documented outside-and-then capability adaptation return to project control at the reviewed boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Hypothesis search and choosing a fresh branch are S1/S3 work on the ongoing research objective, not automatically an S4 strategy function. - Evidence: model-controlled research strategy within task, internal stall search/exploration, catalog-driven project selection. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Parent authority defines the research project&#39;s ultimate purpose and scientific validity/publication boundaries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model-generated proxies or score claims may displace a legitimate user/maintainer research objective, evidence standards or risk limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Set the exact accepted research objective, trusted scorer/evaluator authority and publish/compute mandate; refuse an invented proxy or unapproved external communication. - Decision owner: Project operator (and separately a legitimate upstream maintainer for its own acceptance rules). The model can propose a plan but is constrained by parent-defined objective. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: User-selected project, scoring-authority source pins, immutable run plan and accepted evaluator, spending/score checks and explicit publication review. - Closure path: Operator supplies goal, method limits and accepted scientific rubric; coordinator reads them and writes a matching immutable plan; runner validates candidate outcomes under that rubric and model returns results for owner disposition; following runs reuse the bounded mandate unless the owner changes it. - Boundary reachability: The shipped project UI and method policy demand the user/maintainer criteria and freeze them in the supported method-mode plan before worker launch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Final scientific acceptance and public publication mandate remain with human/project authority; a model&#39;s arbitrary objective proxy does not become ultimate policy. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: What counts as an acceptable contribution or proof for this specific research project, not ordinary per-experiment scheduling. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Parent operator/authorized research rule-owner; no first-party autonomous S5 decision right claimed. - Return-to-operation path: Approved goal and rubric → immutable evaluator/method plan → actual subsequent candidate gate/score and provider tasks → accepted/rejected evidence returned for further project work. - Evidence: user/maintainer scoring authority, preflight rejects invalid initial evidence, user controls research modes and budgets, no unilateral maintainer contact. - Basis: structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/17bc805513918f39d82ff9f9c1699a6907215993/assessments/au/auto-research-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 17bc805513918f39d82ff9f9c1699a6907215993\">17bc805 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T20:37:16+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dtadpole/heddle\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Heddle</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dtadpole/heddle/tree/6ae7e8dd80931c69946b3f1d75d523c77a99252d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6ae7e8dd80931c69946b3f1d75d523c77a99252d\">6ae7e8d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce requested workspace/task results through an iterative model/tool process. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown files, tool observations, permission refusals, new user input and model/context limitations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next tool, arguments, follow-up investigation or final answer in light of returned observations. - Decision owner: the model-driven root/teammate agent running runTurn; the operator supplies its objective and permitted environment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cordis loader, model adapter, context preparation/compaction, tool registry, sandbox, approvals, round limit and append-only session log. - Closure path: agent tool choice → tool output/refusal persisted in session → context prepared for the next model round → revised agent choice or completed result. - Boundary reachability: normal heddle CLI/Web modes use runTurn; the optional Team plugin also calls the same loop for teammate turns. No outside coding-agent CLI is needed for the witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model&#39;s ongoing decisions, deterministic transport and tool handling cannot choose the task-specific next action. - Evidence: loop, Team plugin, profile. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source evidence establishes an executable decision/action path, not model task quality or reliability; no live provider run was made.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate work-claim and ordering conflicts between distinct teammate S1 units on one team board. - Distinct S1 units: Lead and autonomous teammate turns, or two independently running teammates whose work shares the team task board and filesystem. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing claims of one pending task, a stale task update after another teammate&#39;s change, or an attempt to start work before a dependent task is complete. Concurrent write-scope overlap is also surfaced as a warning, not a hard filesystem lock. - Attenuating coordination relation: version-checked claim/update, owner enforcement, dependency readiness, task views with scope-overlap warnings and direct teammate messaging. Agents can choose available work or revise task dependencies/claims after reading the resulting state. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: stale or occupied claims are refused with explicit error codes; the agent receives those tool results, can re-read the board and select a different claim or coordinate with another member before acting. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: compare-and-set revisions and ownership/readiness constraints explicitly address collisions among competing task actors; the returned conflict state changes which task work can begin. The mailbox alone would not suffice. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicting claims, out-of-date plans, blocked dependencies and overlapping teammate work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select/revise task ownership and dependency handling in response to live conflict signals. - Decision owner: Team-mode agents acting through the model-facing task tools; fixed CAS and access checks only enforce their attempts. The Lead also has the supervisory reassign right separately considered under S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable team/task event fold, revision comparison, ownership/lead checks, dependency DAG validation, TEAMTASKSTALE/TEAMTASKBLOCKED refusals and notification/mail transport. - Closure path: multiple teammates read shared work → agent proposes claim/plan → service accepts or refuses based on current conflict/dependency state → result enters agent&#39;s next model round → agent adjusts work selection or message/claim; accepted updates become the next shared task state. - Boundary reachability: the shipped team plugin registers task tools in heddle.patch.yml and wires them to the actual team session log; no developer-only verifier or external coordinator is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents retain choice over which eligible tasks to claim, which conflicts to resolve by messaging and how to revise dependencies; removing their choices leaves only fixed admission guards, not a complete discretionary coordination response. This is a supported optional mode, not a claim that every single-agent run has S2. - Evidence: task state and CAS, model-facing task tools, Team service, policy note. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: write scopes are advisory; source supplies the decision and feedback channel, not an observed conflict-resolution quality result. An operator may direct a team, but no S2 parent notation is used.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain whole-team current commitments and intervene across multiple ongoing task cells. - Whole-system current view: model-facing listagents and teamtasklist expose all members, their live/durable states, task readiness, owners, revisions and dependency blockers. - Current-control decision scope: the Lead can create and revise shared tasks/dependencies, reassign a teammate&#39;s claimed work, reopen work, stop an active teammate turn and send corrective instructions; ordinary teammates cannot reassign someone else&#39;s owned task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an overloaded, blocked, stale or misassigned current team whose shared commitments cannot be resolved within one task cell. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a whole-team assignment/priority/intervention based on the current board and member statuses, including interruption or reallocation. - Decision owner: the model-driven Team Lead in the first-party Team mode. Lead-only role checks and cancellation machinery enforce its chosen action; they do not choose the intervention. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: full team state fold, task ownership and revision gates, requireLead, turn registry and abort signal, serialized message delivery. - Closure path: Lead obtains team-wide status → decides to reassign/reopen/stop/message → Team service updates authoritative task/member operation or aborts a turn → affected teammate receives changed work/steered instruction and subsequent operation follows it. - Boundary reachability: first-party Team plugin registers listagents, teamtasklist, teamtaskupdate, sendmessage and interruptagent, with Lead authority and live turns support wired in the supplied profile. It does not depend on private contributor orchestration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Lead must select which live commitments to change; the runtime merely rejects illegal states and carries the selected change. A team-less profile has no S3 claim from this mechanism. - Evidence: Team service, task intervention rules, Team tools, profile. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: no claim of effective real-world team governance or independent current-performance metrics; hard limits and goal-round continuation alone would not establish S3. Operator instructions over ordinary tasks do not separately prove an S3 parent mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary independent audit-return path established for the installed team/product at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a teammate&#39;s potentially incorrect completion report or misleading task status. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an independently controlled judgment against direct operational evidence and subsequent corrective feedback is not a shipped Team-mode control loop. - Decision owner: the development-side verifier owners govern the separate project-checking process; runtime Lead/teammates can inspect and review but do not instantiate an independently governed complementary auditor by default. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session/event logs, task status, developer verifiers, offline unit tests and review collection scripts. - Closure path: team task completion/status may inform routine Lead decisions; no separately controlled audit-of-reporting decision and return into Team control is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a verifier for Heddle&#39;s own implementation and ordinary self-reported task completion are not independent inspections of teammates&#39; operating reality at this recursion. - Evidence: Team service, verifier boundary, review collection. - Basis: structural, bounded negative. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a user can independently instruct a teammate to inspect raw artifacts, but general tool expressiveness does not establish the function-specific independence and returned control in the shipped operating mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material prospective external-environment sensing, option development and return into installed team capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future model/tool/environment changes rather than current task context or failure recovery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose adaptation of the installation&#39;s future capability based on externally derived options; no such shipped decision path is identified. - Decision owner: operator/developer can update plugins and profiles outside the running organizational loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent session/context, context compaction, goal continuation, endpoint selection and dynamic Cordis plugin loading. - Closure path: goals continue present work and context summaries feed the next task round; neither supplies prospective external intelligence with adopted strategic adaptation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: context and goal maintenance are within-task operations; plugin extensibility is not itself an adaptation regulator. - Evidence: goal driver, context composition, boot/configuration. - Basis: structural, bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: custom plugins or an operator&#39;s separate adaptation program would require a new declared boundary and evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material installed-instance identity or ultimate-policy resolution loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential identity-level tensions about purpose or ultimate permissible operation, distinct from an ordinary tool authorization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: legitimate ultimate-policy judgment with an authoritative return to subsequent operation; not supplied by Heddle&#39;s approval dialogs. - Decision owner: operator selects standing permissions and profiles; project maintainer owns the separate product-development goal, not a shipped identity-level governance loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: allow/deny/ask tool permissions, per-call approval, sandbox modes, configuration file and team-role rules. - Closure path: human approval or denial returns to one tool call; it does not carry an identity/ultimate-policy issue to a legitimate parent and return a durable policy resolution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model cannot revise ultimate identity through a first-party policy-authority path; operator&#39;s ordinary edit/approval is not automatically S5=P. - Evidence: approval service, sandbox policy, development goal. - Basis: structural, bounded negative. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an operator&#39;s real organization can have S5 outside this local installed harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cdef6102b9bb2fd020571ea0486c2be26ad22aec/assessments/dt/dtadpole-heddle.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cdef6102b9bb2fd020571ea0486c2be26ad22aec\">cdef610 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-20T18:29:23+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Marius-Juston/agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agent-harness (Marius-Juston)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-20\">2026-11-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Marius-Juston/agent-harness/tree/2c07a9c07da73bc8c981680a5c08fe9d8ed25f8c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2c07a9c07da73bc8c981680a5c08fe9d8ed25f8c\">2c07a9c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute one assigned code, test or literature research issue and return tangible source artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing project APIs, failing tests, unexpected tool outputs and research findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-backed worker chooses edits, tools, test hypotheses, research sources and response to specific review findings. - Decision owner: Configured Claude Code builder, tester or researcher subagent executing the installed plugin prompt; the third-party model provides inference only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-issue worktree, task brief, tool/secret guards, dev test --affected, dev check, PR check. - Closure path: Worker commits, gates and creates an issue-linked PR; review/merge failure returns source-bound correction into the same issue/worker cycle. - Boundary reachability: Template dev setup installs tagged devkit and plugin; lead calls supplied named subagents, requiring an authenticated external Claude Code install. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static gates do not choose implementation/research actions, while model-backed installed worker does; no model-provider internals are credited. - Evidence: plugins/harness-ops/agents/builder.md; devkit/board.py; docs/specs/multi-agent.md; README.md. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate simultaneous worker interference and competing issue claims across worktrees and machine-local leads. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two workers write overlapping Owns files, claim the same issue, or merge against a stale main. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Admit a disjoint wave, reject duplicate claims and withhold/conflict-return work before integration. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic devkit board/claim/wave and Git conflict logic; leads create scopes but do not autonomously change the anti-conflict policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: GitHub claim comments, issue dependencies, path Owns checks, individual worktrees and merge conflict gates. - Closure path: Conflicting tasks wait or withdrawn claims return to pool; completed/merged tasks release dependencies and rejected conflicting PRs return concrete repair directions. - Boundary reachability: dev board next/wave/claim and dev integrate are shipped, invoked by standard lead skill on every project using all three distribution parts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Inter-worker regulation is source-native but rules are deterministic rather than owned by an adapting S2-specific autonomous actor. - Evidence: devkit/board.py; docs/specs/multi-agent.md; devkit/workflow.py. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Owns approximations do not mediate every resource; GitHub availability is external. - Distinct S1 units: Separate builder/tester/researcher sessions and their independently claimed issues, including workers running on different machines. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Simultaneous writes to shared repository paths and conflicting issue claims disrupt each other&#39;s delivery. - Attenuating coordination relation: board wave excludes overlapping Owns against other in-progress/review work; earliest-live-claim ordering and merge-time checks prevent collisions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Withheld workers are only dispatched once conflict/dependency clears; losing claims withdraw and incompatible PR heads receive worker-specific return notes. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The decision suppresses actual cross-cell write/claim contention, rather than simply sequencing a workflow message.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate the whole current project&#39;s backlog, agent allocation, review, integration and blocked/failed commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent priorities, stalled workers, failed CI, owner-required blockers, budget and quota exhaustion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose what to dispatch, return/re-review, pause, integrate, reprioritize or escalate across many issues. - Decision owner: Model-backed project lead in base mode; legitimate human owner when resolving project-wide priorities/budget and blocked work in parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dev status, dev orient, board next, PR state, dev autopilot, HEAD-aware integrate, HANDOFF and quota cap. - Closure path: Worker/reviewer state returns to lead, whose dispatch, rework, integrate and stop decisions modify board/PR/main; parent owner budget/priority resolutions change subsequent lead resource/commitment choices. - Boundary reachability: Installed /harness-ops:lead, continue and uv run dev supply the operating loop and owner-blocker path without downstream custom control code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Lead discretion concerns whole current commitments, not deterministic scheduling; parent mode has distinct owner authority over project-wide resource constraints, not generic tool approval. - Evidence: plugins/harness-ops/skills/lead/SKILL.md; docs/specs/multi-agent.md; devkit/board.py; devkit/workflow.py. - Basis: structural; explicit protocol - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Human tool permission alone does not establish parent S3; only binding project-wide owner decisions considered. - Whole-system current view: Lead reads open/current review issues, ranked ready work, live claims, quota budget and handoff across all project workers. - Current-control decision scope: Lead can reorganize issue priority, worker assignments, integration and return of failed work across the project. | Mode | Decisive owner | Trigger | Closure | Evidence | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Base (A) | Model lead | New ready/blocked/review issues | Dispatch, return, integrate, change next work across project | skills/lead/SKILL.md | | Parent (P) | Human project owner | Project-wide budget, priority conflict or needs owner | Owner updates governing budget/commitment and lead resumes or stops | docs/specs/multi-agent.md |\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Challenge worker claims against independently inspected Git diff/issue evidence or research sources before integration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Plausible but faulty worker patch, missed DONE WHEN requirement, unsubstantiated note claim or weakened test. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Substantively accept/reject source or research evidence and issue specific blocking findings with a commit-bound verdict. - Decision owner: Distinct installed model-backed read-only reviewer and research-auditor subagents rather than worker or deterministic gate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Diff/commit SHA, quoted review verdict, own issue DONE WHEN, authoritative source retrieval, first-line trusted verdict, bounded rework rounds. - Closure path: Reviewer findings enter PR/lead review; Blocking returns worker for fix and new SHA, success plus gate allows integrate; after repeated failures owner gets blocker. - Boundary reachability: Standard plugin installs both specialized review roles and lead invokes them for user-facing/risky PRs and decision-driving research. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Audit actor separately reads raw source/evidence and decides substantive defects; CI and gate alone only check predefined commands. - Evidence: plugins/harness-ops/agents/reviewer.md; plugins/harness-ops/agents/research-auditor.md; plugins/harness-ops/skills/lead/SKILL.md; devkit/workflow.py. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Reviewer is conditional, no independent empirical quality benchmark, model family can match builder. - Claim being audited: Issue-linked worker claims completed implementation/research is correct, safe and merge-ready. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker dev pr summary, dev check gate and GitHub issue task status. - Complementary access path: Reviewer reads actual gh pr diff and issue + tests, research-auditor re-fetches cited sources and actively seeks contradictory material. - Independence boundary: Dedicated read-only actor cannot edit worker artifact, reviewers are separately tasked and SHA-bound, not a self-reported worker conclusion. - Who acts on findings: Lead and dev integrate return Blocking findings to worker with exact PR feedback, or allow reviewed PR after all checks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No distinct installed prospective external intelligence process for adapting the organization to future environment was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future external scientific, provider, market or operational changes demanding changes to current team capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Would require choosing a future-oriented adaptation from external signals and returning it into current control; no such first-party right is shown. - Decision owner: No dedicated in-boundary S4 owner; research workers own task-level literature retrieval, architect checks internal code architecture. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Literature tools, indexed notes and internal consolidation supply task evidence or internal improvement rather than horizon management. - Closure path: No externally sensed future possibility → proactive choice → present capability adaptation feedback is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Web-enabled task research is S1; codebase consolidation evaluates current internal structure and lacks future-environment return. - Evidence: docs/specs/multi-agent.md; plugins/harness-ops/skills/research/SKILL.md; devkit/consolidation.py. - Basis: bounded source absence - Confidence: medium - Caveats: A user could author future-oriented research issues, but that does not install an ongoing S4 loop. ### Absence scope\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No source-native ultimate organizational identity/policy issue resolution and binding return was demonstrated at local project-team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Identity/purpose conflict between current delivery and future direction, beyond an issue&#39;s scope or immediate resource quota. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Would determine ultimate purpose/policy in response to an identity-level issue and govern later S3/S4. - Decision owner: Human user sets project brief and quotas, but no first-party S5 issue and ultimate-authority transaction is supplied. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLAUDE/project rules, decisions files, board owner blockers and permission guards regulate present tasks. - Closure path: No distinct identity/policy issue → final authority ruling → binding updated team mission feedback is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool permissions, PR merge approval or human-set budget do not constitute an ultimate-policy loop. - Evidence: docs/specs/multi-agent.md; template/.devkit/HARNESS.md.jinja; devkit/rules.py; devkit/board.py. - Basis: bounded source absence - Confidence: medium - Caveats: An adopting enterprise may independently implement S5; its governance cannot be credited to this generic installation. ### Absence scope\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2d3fe927e5708e73feebdb69b4d1ff3279d801bb/assessments/ma/marius-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2d3fe927e5708e73feebdb69b4d1ff3279d801bb\">2d3fe92 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T18:56:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sisyphuslabs/dori-superagent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Dori Superagent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sisyphuslabs/dori-superagent/tree/06c4600b77f6e81ed9bd5b706d736bec07cf27b1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 06c4600b77f6e81ed9bd5b706d736bec07cf27b1\">06c4600 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous implementation and revision of individual written coding tasks by one dedicated agent lane. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unexpected codebase details, changed specifications, test failures, repository state and technical uncertainty within each task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Each independently started omo coding model interprets the brief, chooses code/tool steps, observes their outcomes and can produce further work or a READY evidence report. - Decision owner: Per-lane external omo model hosted in a Dori-created task-specific herdr tab; Dori first-party constructs, starts and wires the lane within its ordinary distribution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Brief+lane-footer prompt, startLane, isolated pane/session, lane report/ready protocol, persistent evidence links and lead return channel. - Closure path: Lead/task brief → first-party startLane() invokes omo worker → model-owned edits/tool observations → report() or evidence-gated ready() → lead receives feedback and can request corrections. - Boundary reachability: dori start is a shipped CLI operation; dori up launches a lead instructed to call it for each user request, and hourly loop also starts eligible pre-authorized briefs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: First-party TS does not decide the code changes; launched agent sessions are the task-specific discretionary actors, although the underlying omo tool engine is external. - Evidence: harness.ts#L130-L188, lane-footer.md, lead.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high for shipped first-party session constructor, medium for provider runtime success without live provider execution. - Caveats: Agent cognition and terminal implementation come from herdr/omo; no claim of ownership of their standalone agent features.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Distinct S1 units: Two or more independently briefed worker lanes are separate omo sessions in separate herdr tabs, identified by unique lane keys, and can send messages to each other with dori send &lt;key&gt; --text ...; the lead is a separate receiving/forwarding role. - Inter-S1 disturbance: A message or handoff emitted while another lane is actively executing a tool/model turn can interrupt that S1&#39;s own evidence-to-action closure, especially with several concurrently operating workers and multiple queued requests. - Attenuating coordination relation: First-party per-pane mailbox stores every message, checks destination agent readiness through herdr and the screen guard, and defers/batches cross-lane deliveries until the target returns to idle; the duty loop retries delivery and watches lost worker panes. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: On the next idle observation, flushInbox() injects a queued bundle into the recipient lane&#39;s model input; the recipient then incorporates the sender&#39;s reports/instructions on its next coding turn. A stalled/lost worker additionally triggers lead intervention rather than blind repeated prompting. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The readiness-dependent non-interruption relation explicitly regulates timing interference between simultaneous independent S1 sessions and returns deferred information only when each S1 can integrate it; ordinary address routing alone would deliver immediately without checking the working/blocked agent state. - Function: Regulate interference from cross-lane/lead/duty messages arriving when a particular agent pane is busy, plus stale or vanished lane liveness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Interruption of working agents by asynchronous messages, competing pane readiness, delayed event reports and unobserved lost lanes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party constructor chooses whether to deliver immediately, queue until idle, batch, nudge or escalate based on explicit pane feedback and fixed policies; the duty model can additionally unblock WAITING cases but is not automatically summoned for every such decision. - Decision owner: send/flushInbox and dutyTick first-party TS policies; not an independent reliably closed autonomous S2 agent for each conflict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Herdr agentstatus plus screen-idle guard, persistent per-pane inbox, idle thresholds 10/20/40 minutes, lost-pane detection, stop/resume and lead messages. - Closure path: Lane/role message → observe destination pane state → queue if busy → duty polling observes idle → batch deliver; stale/lost reports → fixed nudge and escalation → lead can intervene. - Boundary reachability: dori send calls send() in ordinary use; dori up creates a duty loop pane and dutyTick for periodic inbox flushing and lane status. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: S2 coordination is a concrete first-party constructor, but scheduling thresholds and delivery choice are code policies; optional duty-agent unblocking does not prove general independent model control of the feedback loop. - Evidence: harness.ts#L85-L110, harness.ts#L225-L283, duty.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for constructor and state return; medium for autonomous optional duty intervention. - Caveats: herdr is the external source of status; code itself mitigates interruption rather than proving fault-free scheduling across machines.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: The lead receives [START], [REPORT], [READY], [LOST], [ESCALATE] per independent worker lane, plus [LOOP] summaries with open lane count, backlog status, host capacity and stale-work findings; persistent lane/store records support a whole-current operational picture. - Current-control decision scope: The configured lead model can create new briefs and assign them to separate lanes, redirect existing workers, decide whether to rebrief/restart lost work, judge READY evidence and authorize exact-head reviewed merges/closures while escalating product/irreversible choices to the human. - Function: Lead-level current task and resource control of the whole multi-lane delivery operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Simultaneous task commitments, user changes, blocked worker lanes, uncertain progress, failed reviews, stale ready tasks and host capacity limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-driven lead assigns a brief to a new lane, examines ready evidence, decides next correction/restart/rebrief/escalation, and determines when to merge/close; human reserves irreversible/product decisions. - Decision owner: First-party prompted, launched omo LEAD session; concrete resource bounds/backlog scheduling are separately enforced by deterministic TS loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: prompts/lead.md, persistent lane logs/briefs, lead status/READY/LOST/ESCALATE messages, dori start, send, done, hourly capacity/load and decision gates. - Closure path: Whole-current lane and task reports → first-party lead inbox → model decides launch/adjust/retry/close → CLI mutation changes lane state or directs workers → subsequent reports return to lead. - Boundary reachability: dori up starts the lead omo with role prompt in supported CLI path; tellLead delivers lane and duty signals to its pane. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The lead decides task-specific cross-lane interventions beyond a static queue; quota and git gates are enforcement, not the basis for the A grade. - Evidence: cli.ts#L70-L87, lead.md, harness.ts#L116-L127, loop.ts#L310-L353. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high for decision right and reachable constructor; not a live throughput or guarantee-of-success assessment. - Caveats: No inference that automated hourly loop alone is S3=A; lead model is required for that autonomy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: A worker&#39;s implementation/PR is ready to merge at a specific head SHA, over and above its self-report and ordinary CI. - Ordinary reporting path: Lane ready() carries final text and evidence link to lead; CI provides test-check status separately. - Complementary access path: review() reads live PR head, instantiates a fresh non-interactive omo reviewer with --no-session and role-specific prompt instructing it to read the PR/diff, then parses CLEAR/BLOCK and stores a head-keyed review receipt. - Independence boundary: Separate reviewer session and fresh prompt inspect the head&#39;s PR diff rather than accepting the worker&#39;s narrative; the same configurable model/provider might underlie both roles, so institutional/model-provider independence is not proven. - Who acts on findings: Reviewer prints actionable HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW findings and BLOCK/CLEAR; lead can direct remediation, and mergeGate() refuses a missing/BLOCK/stale receipt or red/absent checks. - Function: Independent first-party delegated source/diff challenge with hard return to the admission/merge path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect code despite worker claims, untested regressions, security defects, scope creep and post-review PR mutation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Dedicated review model autonomously judges evidence/failures against goal and emits binding verdict consumed by an exact-head merge gate. - Decision owner: Fresh first-party-constructed reviewer omo agent for its audit verdict; deterministic git/check/receipt guard enforces the returned outcome. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: prompts/review.md, omo -p --no-session, head SHA capture, store.saveReceipt, gh pr checks, --match-head-commit merge. - Closure path: PR HEAD → new reviewer reads PR/diff → CLEAR/BLOCK recorded for SHA → lead/merge reads receipt and CI → block/repair/re-review or authorized exact-head merge. - Boundary reachability: Normal user-visible dori review owner/repo#N CLI path, and dori merge requires its receipt; this is not repository-development CI only. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Independent model audit decision is separate from the implementer and first-party harness explicitly invokes it; CI checks are additional evidence, not the claimed autonomous auditor. - Evidence: harness.ts#L289-L355, review.md, store.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high for constructor, independent session and return gate; actual detection quality not measured. - Caveats: The reviewer could share provider/model and GitHub access with the worker; A does not assert independent external operator or S3 audit effectiveness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No distinct forward-looking environmental intelligence→option selection→durable change in present organizational capability demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New user backlog tickets, issue status and host load are immediately actionable current work signals rather than longer-horizon environmental perturbations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Hourly loop chooses next eligible authorized task by static priority/capacity, and lead can prioritize work, but no first-party future strategy/adaptation right is established. - Decision owner: Operator/lead choose what enters backlog; deterministic hourly loop executes present scheduling. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Import from labeled GitHub issues, backlog priorities, host load gates, task aging, retries, sync and stop controls. - Closure path: Backlog/host observation → current lane start/held report; no separate prospective environmental option enters a capability-change decision and returns to S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Proactive scheduling and continuous task liveness do not by themselves constitute a prospective strategic S4 feedback loop. - Evidence: loop.ts#L310-L429, hourly-loop.md, lead.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high at selected recursion. - Caveats: Long-term product decisions by a human or software maintenance outside runtime are excluded.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No explicit closed ultimate-policy/identity decision process for the Dori organization, though human control and static boundaries are prominent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Irreversible decisions, product-scope changes, unsafe merges and unsupported work are controlled by permission/approval prompts and merge gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Lead must ask human for irreversible/product decisions; the repository does not construct a binding mission/policy authority with a returned organization-wide policy-revision loop. - Decision owner: Human operator outside the shipped autonomous policy process; developer-specified prompts and code enforce fixed gates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Human approval rule in lead.md, bounded backlog decision queue, README one-voice and no-unasked-work rules, stale-review and CI fail-closed gates. - Closure path: Question to human → human answer may enter backlog brief; this is local work authorization, not necessarily policy/identity revision for the whole system. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Respecting a parent&#39;s task authorization is not in itself a first-party ultimate-policy S5 process. - Evidence: lead.md, loop.ts#L65-L72, README. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high for human gate, medium for scoped absence. - Caveats: Human and organization may exercise a parent S5 outside this public runtime, which is not inferred or scored.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/fd763ded09bc47ddfbd64aa26c541920dcf48ec3/assessments/do/dori-superagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit fd763ded09bc47ddfbd64aa26c541920dcf48ec3\">fd763de &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T18:56:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DMRLZZ/DaveCode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DaveCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DMRLZZ/DaveCode/tree/271910253a005845174c2410dc15675877a9883e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 271910253a005845174c2410dc15675877a9883e\">2719102 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute user-directed coding and goal/artifact work through model-owned next-action decisions and actual workspace/output feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varying repository state, implementation alternatives, task dependencies, real tool outputs, failed quality gates, incomplete context, provider constraints and uncompleted goal artifacts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which first-party coding tools to invoke and what code/action to attempt next; for artifact roles, generate a task-specific output from received objective and dependency evidence. - Decision owner: the selected model-backed BuiltinExecutor actor and goal role agents in supported first-party mode. The runner selects/validates task transitions deterministically and does not inherit the model&#39;s coding discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Router, quota/budget, WorkspaceTools, token/iteration ceilings, Git branch operations, task context, retry/repair policy, goal store and acceptance validators. - Closure path: current task/context → model requests repository tools → WorkspaceTools executes → exact tool result enters same conversation → next coding decision; goal artifact calls persist their output and return it to the subsequent goal review/task selection. - Boundary reachability: createRunner constructs the shipped built-in executor; wireProject exposes it through CLI/server; GoalOrchestrator.runCodeTask invokes the first-party runner while artifact work calls router.complete with first-party role prompts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed code/role actor leaves graph selection, router/failover and verification machinery, but no discretionary construction of task-specific code edits or artifacts from evidence. - Evidence: executor.ts; runner.ts; orchestrator.ts; runtime.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for shipped source construction; no provider-backed outcomes measured. - Caveats: only the native builtin composition supplies this positive S1 path. External CLI executor modes may have their own decision owner outside DaveCode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate competing operations&#39; shared-worktree mutation conflict between the first-party standalone autonomous runner and first-party goal operation that invokes code tasks on the same project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: both top-level execution modes check out Git branches and commit in one writable workspace; simultaneous use would race on branch/index/worktree state. - Distinct S1 units: the standalone AutonomousRunner coding unit and the goal-directed role/code operational composition exposed as a separate first-party GoalService. They are distinct runnable operational entry points to one repository, not two model calls within the same tool step. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing top-level runner and goal ownership of one Git worktree can cause Git checkout/commit interference. workspace-lock.ts explicitly identifies this conflict. - Attenuating coordination relation: wireProject instantiates one shared WorkspaceLock; the runner acquires it as owner runner, goal mode holds it as goal, a competing owner receives busy, and nested goal-owned code runner uses reentrant goal ownership. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: rejected launch returns RunnerError(&#39;busy&#39;)/GoalError(&#39;busy&#39;) to the parent CLI/API caller, preventing conflicting execution; subsequent initiation must occur after the holder releases the lock. A model-owned cross-unit rescheduler is not instantiated. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: this is not a DAG edge or role routing: a first-party lock is explicitly tied to a real shared-Git mutation disturbance between two separately startable coding/goal operating units and changes their admission behaviour. - Decisive decision or feedback right: grant or refuse exclusive entry to the shared writable-workspace operating boundary and report the conflict for later caller scheduling. - Decision owner: deterministic WorkspaceLock enforces exclusivity according to fixed rules; a developer/operator would have to compose an autonomous policy for what postponed work to start next. The shipped S2-specific feedback path supports constructor state C, not autonomous A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ownership-counting WorkspaceLock; shared wireProject runtime wiring; runner acquireLock and GoalService.start; goal RunnerCodeTasks reentrant owner. - Closure path: goal/runner start proposal → shared lock grants or rejects → only one writer progresses; rejection returns busy to caller and later attempts may proceed after release. Autonomous adaptive cross-unit scheduling remains to be composed. - Boundary reachability: the published CLI project wiring always shares the lock between the first-party goal and standalone runner for that project; this does not depend on an unshipped plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the conflict-specific first-party lock enforces a fixed partition and returns a decision, but a coding model does not autonomously decide who waits, retries or reallocates operations after a collision. - Evidence: workspace-lock.ts; goal/service.ts; goal/code-tasks.ts; cli/runtime.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; the claim is limited to exclusive shared-worktree admission, not rich semantic task coordination. - Caveats: lock is in-process and does not prove cross-process or distributed exclusion. Alternative top-level execution modes may be seen as exclusive rather than co-active S1s; this is the principal semantic boundary to review before canonical admission.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate the present whole-goal task commitment population through reviewer-informed retries/new tasks and operator-managed task-graph priorities/constraints. - Disturbance / variety regulated: some goal tasks fail or become blocked, artifact/code outcomes vary, budget/task/round limits constrain current work and changed task priorities may require revised commitment selection. - Whole-system current view: goal reviewer receives every task&#39;s status, role, attempt count, title, notes and bounded actual artifact excerpt, plus budget context and current round. The CLI tasks listing and API expose the shared project task graph to operators. - Current-control decision scope: the goal reviewer decides when to retry failed work, which additional tasks enter the next round, and whether the bounded entire goal is complete. In operator mode, current project task priority/dependency/acceptance edits and status changes alter which pending commitment nextTask selects in later runner iterations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: revise current task commitments from whole-goal evidence and return the decision to task execution, or let legitimate project operator do so through task-graph control. - Decision owner: goal reviewer model actor for the autonomous A mode; project operator using first-party task list/edit/status API for the distinct P mode. Fixed task/round/token limits enforce bounds but do not own the discretionary choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: GoalOrchestrator.review, applyRetries/normalizeTasks, persisted GoalStore, task graph validation, nextTask, CLI/HTTP routes and fixed budgets/workspace locks. - Closure path: goal outcome/status/artifact excerpts → reviewer model produces retry/new task/finish judgment → orchestrator alters tasks and executes the next round. Separately, operator reads task graph → changes priority/dependencies/status → persisted project graph → next runner selection consumes the revised commitments. - Boundary reachability: first-party GoalService is constructed by createGoalService and wired into the published CLI/runtime; task listing/edit endpoints and runner graph reads are part of the same installed project mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the goal reviewer the deterministic DAG/limit machinery can walk given tasks but cannot make evidence-driven judgments to revise the current goal task portfolio. In operator mode the human makes those current-control decisions; the API only stores/enforces them. - Evidence: goal/orchestrator.ts; goal/service.ts; cli/commands/tasks.ts; server/routes/tasks.ts; brain/graph.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded goal-level S3; the operator mode is project-current-control only, not a claim of global DaveCode governance. - Caveats: a standalone runner&#39;s deterministic priority choice and stop button alone are not the autonomous S3 basis. The positive autonomous mode rests on the goal reviewer receiving whole-goal status and revising current commitments, not merely task decomposition.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: supply an optional independent acceptance judgment over actual staged code changes beyond the production actor&#39;s summary and configured deterministic check results, with corrective return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: coding actor may self-report completion despite a patch failing the task&#39;s acceptance criteria even when configured lints/tests pass. - Claim being audited: that the implementation diff fulfills each task acceptance criterion and can be accepted/merged. - Ordinary reporting path: built-in executor returns its summary and the Validator runs configured checks; without an independent configured judge, NoneJudge always passes after the checks. - Complementary access path: when runner.judge.kind=&#39;jev&#39; the first-party runner obtains actual staged Git diff/stat plus test validation, passes them and task acceptance to dedicated TypeSafe Jev via JevJudge, and receives a separate calibrated acceptance verdict. The alternative llm judge exposes the same inputs but its routing independence is not assumed. - Independence boundary: the Jev decision is made by an external dedicated evaluator on fresh raw Git diff/validation evidence with a separate model/service from the coding actor&#39;s implementation session, not by replaying the implementer&#39;s own success message. Source-level evidence supports the path; no live independence/accuracy experiment was run. - Who acts on findings: AutonomousRunner converts a failed judge verdict into a failure report for the subsequent implementation repair cycle, or marks the task FAILED when the configured retry limit is reached. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge whether the actual change satisfies acceptance criteria independently of the coding actor&#39;s conclusion; determine acceptance or corrective retry. - Decision owner: the optional dedicated TypeSafe Jev evaluator model in the first-party jev supported mode; DaveCode&#39;s schema parser, confidence threshold, and runner enforce/transport its judgment without owning the evaluator&#39;s discretionary assessment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: createJudge, acceptance inputs/diff capture, score threshold, zod response validation, validator, repair-pass state machine and bounded failure handling. - Closure path: candidate diff/test results → independent Jev assessment → pass reaches delivery or reject is incorporated into failure text → built-in coding agent sees report and repairs → fresh diff is rejudged. - Boundary reachability: createRunner selects createJudge(cfg.judge, ...) from first-party runner config, which permits jev; runTask invokes it after successful deterministic validation as part of installed CLI/goal-driven coding operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the separate evaluator owns the acceptance judgment, while the runner and threshold gate enforce the returned verdict; it is not ordinary self-review by the production actor in this supported mode. - Evidence: autonomous/judge.ts; autonomous/create.ts; autonomous/runner.ts; config/schema.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; optional independent judgment/return is clearly wired, but practical audit quality was not tested. - Caveats: default judge.kind=&#39;none&#39; has no independent audit; an llm judge on the same model route cannot be assumed independent. The A state credits the explicit first-party opt-in dedicated Jev mode only and does not imply all deployments instantiate it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party external-and-prospective organizational capability adaptation loop established within the supported project runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: quota exhaustion, model availability and current-task failures are detected; internal replanning/repair and operational failover respond to present conditions, not outside-and-then organizational capability evolution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate prospectively sourced option to redesign current organizational capability with an adoption decision was established. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: quota windows, provider route scoring/circuit breakers, goal plan/review rounds, retry/repair feedback and global/project memory. - Closure path: no externally/future informed capability-option generation → judgment/adoption → return into current organizational capability is wired; immediate failover and goal replanning return only into current operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: role planner and goal reviewer plan/revise task actions, while router algorithms choose among configured providers; none is evidenced as autonomous prospective redesign of DaveCode&#39;s capability repertoire. - Evidence: docs/ARCHITECTURE.md; goal/orchestrator.ts; engine.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high within the frozen source distribution. - Caveats: tracking provider quotas over future windows is predictive operational routing, not evidence of a distinct S4 capability-adaptation organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop established at the declared project harness boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: goal text, route settings, allowed tools, account policy, branch/merge modes, acceptance and operator task edits constrain execution but do not decide harness ultimate identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity/ultimate-policy issue is escalated, decided by legitimate authority and returned as a durable authoritative runtime policy revision. - Decision owner: not established for S5; operator/developer configuration sets constraints rather than closing a distinct VSM S5 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DaveConfig, DAVECODESYSTEMPROMPT, CLI/API task edits, security checks, route settings, scoped workspaces and execution limits. - Closure path: no identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned policy governing later operation is instantiated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planner/reviewer/task owner decisions concern the task portfolio, not the identity of the coding organization; static config and permission gates do not exercise S5 authority by themselves. - Evidence: config/schema.ts; goal/orchestrator.ts; server/routes/tasks.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high within runtime boundary. - Caveats: this negative is not a statement about maintainer governance for future DaveCode releases.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e1e4706466182cc143e13deb1db6b560aa529b3d/assessments/da/davecode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e1e4706466182cc143e13deb1db6b560aa529b3d\">e1e4706 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T15:03:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tracedelange/orca\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orca (Trace DeLange)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tracedelange/orca/tree/bfdff98bdcc5e3bee5eecd790f00eb8f1104776f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision bfdff98bdcc5e3bee5eecd790f00eb8f1104776f\">bfdff98 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: attached Claude sessions autonomously perform changing project work, edit repository artifacts and report results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-specific code state, requirements, tests, model tool feedback and follow-up user/parent messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each worker chooses source operations and task edits, and can revise its work on a parent or conflict prompt. - Decision owner: Claude Code agent session launched/claimed/queued by Orca; deterministic server/hook do not invent coding decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: node goal/context inheritance, tmux or SSH session host, installed hooks, Claude tool runtime, queue worktree and viewer. - Closure path: worker receives its node brief, acts and emits Status/Done; server or parent reads its result and may return corrections or archive it. - Boundary reachability: orca up installs the Claude integration and orca launch, dispatch, queue and claim are first-party installed paths to running model agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: active wired Claude sessions make variable project decisions; status hooks and static node files only support them. - Evidence: README CLI and queue, session launch, hook. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this review did not run a paid Claude session or a live macOS launchd server.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: manage conflicting parallel S1 worker changes in one repository by isolated worktrees and a bounded model-backed merge-repair loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two workers independently edit overlapping code and the merge into the shared branch conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: when git identifies exact conflicted paths, server returns those paths and the current base into the responsible worker, which chooses a semantic combined edit; server retries merge once or marks failure. - Decision owner: queued Claude worker makes the repair choice in an explicitly invoked first-party conflict-resolution mode; deterministic queue manager owns isolation, conflict trigger, refusal and retry limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-child git worktrees/branches, two-worker cap, first-party queue state, git merge/abort, send to the exact worker pane and retried map. - Closure path: worker A merges; worker B&#39;s conflict aborts cleanly, base merges into B worktree and B receives a prompt to preserve both intents; on next turn the server commits/retries, succeeds or records failed task after second conflict. - Boundary reachability: the queue manager is a default first-party server loop for enabled queue tasks and directly launches/returns to Claude worktree workers. No external conflict-solver plugin is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: specific cross-S1 conflict recovery is wired to an autonomous worker choosing the semantic resolution, not simply a static git check; server bounds and schedules the intervention. - Distinct S1 units: two independently running queue workers, each in its own branch/worktree for one node&#39;s backlog. - Inter-S1 disturbance: both independently modify the same repository path, creating a merge conflict on integration. - Attenuating coordination relation: isolated worktrees plus precise conflict handback to the affected worker, with one bounded corrective attempt. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: worker receives real conflicted file names and a concrete instruction to keep both changes, and its next output re-enters the merge loop. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the queue relates two actual operational streams competing over shared git state and closes a specific coordination conflict with corrective feedback. - Evidence: queue start/finish/repair, design queue sequence, queue integration tests. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: no unlimited mediation: second conflict fails, and the manager may merge a worker that stopped after an input question; recovery is bounded to queue mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: inspect and adjust the current structure of a whole work subtree by classifying stale/completed/live nodes, proposing moves/archives and returning a scoped change through explicit human acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: abandoned experiments, context clutter, redundant folders and confusing current node-to-task ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-backed consolidator selects node classes and proposes specific current restructuring; legitimate human first approves the proposal, and then accepts/rejects the actual subtree changes. - Decision owner: separate Claude consolidator agent for the base scoped current-control judgment and action; human node-tree owner for the parent authority to authorize and accept/reject changes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: full subtree node/Log/Todo read, class/action template, live-session exclusion, before-snapshot git commit, nested round lock, finishRound accept/reject and browser dialog. - Closure path: operator invokes consolidate, Claude studies complete subtree and drafts proposal; user approves, agent moves/archives/updates active structure, user accepts (commit) or rejects (revert scoped change), lock releases and structure returns to subsequent sessions. - Boundary reachability: orca consolidate &lt;path&gt; and the browser Consolidate action are shipped, the prompt is installed in templates/consolidate.md, and app/server expose explicit round lifecycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-backed consolidator decides how the present operational context should be structured and enacts approved changes; daemon and human approvals constrain rather than replace its situational judgment. - Whole-system current view: consolidator is instructed to read all node CLAUDE.md, Logs, TODOs and docs in its selected subtree; at root, that is the entire local node organization, with live nodes identified. - Current-control decision scope: archive dead/stub/completed work, preserve long-running nodes, re-group context and return the altered present work structure to active agents. - Evidence: consolidation template, round lifecycle, viewer endpoint, root agent conventions. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: S3 is explicitly invoked by a human, not continuous autonomous whole-work oversight; the routine queue manager on its own is not credited with discretionary S3.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no default independent direct-evidence auditor validates workers&#39; results separately from their ordinary Status/Done reports. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false completion or bad merged code is possible, but an independent audit review-return policy is not packaged. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none for a distinct auditor; human may open transcripts or inspect uncommitted counts, while queue manager merges when the worker stops. - Decision owner: active worker reports its own work, daemon operates merge gate and human can inspect; no separately wired independent reviewer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session hook/status, terminal viewer, git status --porcelain warning, queue conflict checks and manual source access. - Closure path: worker completion triggers routine queue merge, or operator views its session; no independent raw-diff finding leads to a mandatory corrective auditor/worker loop before admission. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: git merge conflict is S2 resource coordination and ordinary work status is S1/S3 reporting, neither independently judges operational truth. - Evidence: queue merge implementation, hook and viewer contract. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a user can manually request Claude code review, but that custom task is not a first-party complementary audit gate.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective outside-environment sensing/option-generation/adaptation-to-current-work loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new demands and future projects can be queued as parked ideas, but Orca does not itself transform external trend evidence into future strategy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct future-intelligence owner or organizational adaptation decision. - Decision owner: operator supplies goals, ideas, future tasks; dispatch/consolidation model decisions place or tidy existing work rather than evaluate externally grounded future scenarios. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-node TODO.md, dispatch placement, queue, contextual history, visual subtree map and optional skill-created diagram. - Closure path: a person may queue a parked idea, but no automatic outside/future signal-to-capability-shift loop returns to S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: future-dated notes, graph visualization and stale-node cleanup are present-context tools, not independently driven environment intelligence. - Evidence: design Todo and Not built/Future work, built-in skill. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: users may ask project agents to research the future; no standing first-party S4 procedure is demonstrated.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: the node tree carries goals and a single-user root context, but no system-level identity/ultimate-policy deliberation with legitimate decision and operational return is packaged. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user preferences, context naming and operation permission are configuration/current-control issues, not necessarily organizational ethos tensions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party ultimate policy process; human owns roots, terminal permissions and node structure. - Decision owner: the user may choose changes as current owner; the software does not model an S5-specific authority resolving identity-policy conflicts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: root CLAUDE.md, human consent on consolidation, local-only server and implicit single-user trust assumptions. - Closure path: root edits and approvals affect context and current nodes; no identity/policy issue escalates through a distinct forum to binding S5 return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: hierarchical goals, root context and ownership are not sufficient to evidence the highest policy/identity function. - Evidence: root conventions, design one-user boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: particular user&#39;s root notes could contain meaningful policy, but that external content was not reviewed or claimed as Orca-owned S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/aa914acccd98baf4333cf2a8fdd84195c8a3c606/assessments/or/orca-trace-delange.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit aa914acccd98baf4333cf2a8fdd84195c8a3c606\">aa914ac &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T14:46:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Shredi/pi-foreman\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pi-foreman</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Shredi/pi-foreman/tree/8f8c473cc2332602373d03850d4826eea2f79f51\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8f8c473cc2332602373d03850d4826eea2f79f51\">8f8c473 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-backed builder agents execute variable repository coding tasks, inspect/test their changes and return artifacts to the foreman. - Disturbance / variety regulated: source state, unexpected errors, requested behavior, tests and revision instructions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select concrete code edits and tests within a delegated ledger item, then revise after failed review. - Decision owner: configured Pi subagent builder model, acting through the package&#39;s role definition and scoped tool runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pi tools, run binding, role model maps, per-role permissions, git and shell guards, launch/read budgets. - Closure path: foreman launches builder with item/brief, builder changes code/tests and reports, review returns findings, builder can perform corrective revision. - Boundary reachability: standard install explicitly provisions the builder role and the foreman&#39;s launch mechanism; user asks for a task, not a custom role integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: builders choose coding actions in a wired model/tool session; guards constrain them without owning the coding decisions. - Evidence: role protocol, ceremony, launch binding. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: code execution was not benchmarked in this review; the trivial path may omit reviewer by its bounded exception.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate accidental shared-checkout collisions when multiple operational builders/sessions use related repository work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent writers may change the same working tree or push from unauthorized session/branch. - Decisive decision or feedback right: worktree/session binding and guard refuse a child write/push outside its owned checkout; the foreman can compose a different task/worktree and sequence follow-up. - Decision owner: deterministic gate and worktree ownership checks; any choice to split conflicting tasks or coordinate a new branch remains with foreman/human composition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-launch worktrees, child-bound cwd, shell-write guard, git push/branch ownership and session handoff metadata. - Closure path: a prohibited operation is refused with a reason; the foreman may give the builder an owned worktree or serialize the conflicting edit, then retry the changed task. - Boundary reachability: worktree launching and guards are shipped first-party options on top of directly integrated child roles. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a function-specific guard prevents concrete shared-resource interference, but dynamic multi-builder conflict negotiation and repair do not have an installed autonomous S2 owner. - Distinct S1 units: separate builder agents or full pi-foreman child sessions operating in parallel. - Inter-S1 disturbance: a non-owning child trying to change a checkout or push a branch while another session works there. - Attenuating coordination relation: owned-worktree isolation and per-session branch/checkout checks, with explicit refusal. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: refusal directs a model/human to a permitted checkout or rescheduling; no automatic agent-side conflict mediation is guaranteed. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the checks enforce particular cross-agent write/checkout contention, beyond message transport or generic task ordering. - Evidence: worktree session setup, worktree boundary, safety. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: no general merge-conflict resolution or automatic global scheduling is established; the session registry explicitly lacks a transaction lock.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: convert current user task to scoped ledger commitments, choose helper roles and tiers, review progress, authorize iteration and close the task only after required evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: overlarge/unverified changes, blocked child work, missing tests, stale reviews and non-aligned PRs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: foreman chooses task decomposition/launch/revision and can issue corrected work; in a heavy plan mode the human can veto or approve the precise reviewed plan hash before execution. - Decision owner: the foreman agent in normal mode; the owner at the explicit heavy-plan checkpoint, under a separate parent intervention rather than an agent approval. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tier ledger, plan/revision counters, finish gate, reviewer marks, model ladder, checkpoint hash, PR head validation. - Closure path: foreman receives child completions/verdicts, updates ledger and launches revisions; exact review gates constrain finish. Heavy mode first returns plan to owner and only an approved version opens builder launch. - Boundary reachability: standard installed foreman protocol and role extensions include both the autonomous current-control path and the explicit human checkpoint tool. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses plans/assignments/revisions; deterministic checks enforce minimum ceremony. Heavy-plan release is intentionally and demonstrably human-owned. - Whole-system current view: task ledger, active child runs, reviewer findings, cost/session state and bound current plan. - Current-control decision scope: task tier, delegated item ownership/ordering, revision requests, review closure and completion. - Evidence: ceremony and checkpoint, current-control gate, plan review. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a default task has no human checkpoint, and a gate is not itself discretionary S3. Incomplete-review escape after repeated refusals is explicit and visible.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: a separate read-only reviewer agent examines raw builder changes/tests against ledger scope and returns findings/verification before a PR. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a builder may incorrectly declare a work item done, introduce regressions or miss required tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reviewer reports per-item PASS/FAIL and located findings from diff/source/test inspection; review gate requires current valid PASS for PR and ordinary standard completion. - Decision owner: the distinct reviewer subagent with independent read-only access to repository artifacts and ledger; foreman acts on the verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewer role deny-write tool set, ledger scope injection, different-model rule where available, head-bound review records, re-review item list and PR guard. - Closure path: reviewer receives independently sourced code/diff context, returns item findings/verdict; foreman delegates correction; rerun review validates changed head; guard returns pass/fail to promotion. - Boundary reachability: installed reviewer role is part of standard required ceremony and the PR gate, not just a suggested external review service. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a separate model-backed child observes direct operational artifacts rather than relying solely on builder&#39;s claimed completion, and its findings return through an actual corrective gate. - Claim being audited: builder&#39;s ledger-item completion and correctness of modified repository artifacts. - Ordinary reporting path: builder&#39;s completion message and foreman&#39;s ledger. - Complementary access path: distinct reviewer tool session inspecting raw checkout/diff/tests under its own read-only role, with scoped ledger injected. - Independence boundary: role and run separated from builder, its write tools denied; reviewer model differs from foreman when available, but a distinct model from builder is not absolutely guaranteed. - Who acts on findings: foreman requests builder revision; head-aware review/PR gate blocks promotion until valid verdict (subject to documented finish exceptions). - Evidence: review protocol, review marks, git/PR guard. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: reviewing the same model family weakens cognitive independence, no guarantee of a successful audit outcome or fully independent external organization; trivial bounded tasks need not launch reviewer.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no standard first-party loop from external future environment evidence to prospective capability choices and returned current-control adaptation was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool/provider shifts or future project demands could need adaptation, but the shipped system mostly reacts to current tasks and internal process friction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no dedicated externally sensing prospective planner chooses future options and feeds an adaptation decision into S3. - Decision owner: owner can change role models and policies, and the foreman may record retro hints, but neither is an evidenced installed S4 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /retro summaries, trace-based permission-map proposals, model ladder/presets, cost/bench metrics. - Closure path: /retro proposes permission rules and warns that the owner must apply them manually; no grounded outside/future option-to-adapt loop is wired by default. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: retrospective telemetry and model selection optimize present workflow from past self-traces, not a distinct prospective environmental intelligence function. - Evidence: retrospective boundary, design/architecture. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a user may explicitly ask the foreman for research/future strategy, but generic model capacity does not establish the distributed S4 contract.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: package exposes human authorization and configuration preferences but no organizational identity/ultimate-policy deliberation and authoritative return procedure. - Disturbance / variety regulated: sensitive operations, protected branches, secrets and child permissions require safety constraints, not necessarily a whole-organization ethos dispute. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human decides asks/plan approval and sets permission policy, but no installed S5-specific identity issue or legitimate policy forum processes such issues. - Decision owner: human owner and deterministic safety gate exercise task/security authority, not an evidenced separate S5 policy identity loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission whitelist, hard floors, protected-file ownership, heavy checkpoint and child-ask flows. - Closure path: a decision to allow/deny a tool or approve a plan affects current execution; no S5 issue -&gt; identity policy resolution -&gt; returned organizational governance path is packaged. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: human involvement is not automatically S5, nor does a role policy and permissions file itself express the VSM identity-policy function. - Evidence: permissions, safety, checkpoint. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: site-level organizational rules can be installed via overlays, but those are not a first-party ultimate-policy loop by themselves.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/68e768215984cff45d2210b8cd9f74e52423490d/assessments/pi/pi-foreman.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 68e768215984cff45d2210b8cd9f74e52423490d\">68e7682 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-11T21:14:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sailry/sailry-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sailry</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-11\">2026-11-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sailry/sailry-harness/tree/eaad1b7f18e448c8c4e9e9286e19560c8a351ae1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision eaad1b7f18e448c8c4e9e9286e19560c8a351ae1\">eaad1b7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-controlled user task execution over first-party project, terminal, browser and other tool surfaces. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing repository contents, tool failures, ambiguous requests, project-specific instructions and provider responses. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses next available tool and arguments from current conversation and returned observations, including when to finish or delegate in supported modes. - Decision owner: model operating within Sailry&#39;s first-party Host agent session; platform sets tool and permission limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model integration, tool catalog, captured modes and permissions, persistent session state, execution workers, approval flow. - Closure path: user/host session task → model reasoning → first-party tool execution → observed result/error → subsequent model choice or final response. - Boundary reachability: documented Create session on local/remote Host starts the shipped Node runtime, rather than outsourcing the loop to an external coding harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model owns discretionary tool/action selection inside developer/user constraints. - Evidence: README Host loop and features, Node execution, session/project operation. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source-backed capability, not a live provider/API end-to-end exercise.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among concurrently working child S1 agents by assigning non-overlapping writable scopes and sequencing shared integration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing child edits to the same files, shared module lists/manifests and test runs on inconsistent integrated output. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Flagship parent model chooses a division into disjoint owned files or worktrees, retains shared registration files, delegates roles, and reassigns follow-up fixes based on observed conflicts and consolidated checks. - Decision owner: the first-party Flagship parent model, instructed by a shipped coordination policy; bounded child admission is deterministic infrastructure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: spawnagent task brief with owned paths, optional registered worktrees, guideagent, child result collection, concurrency budget and integration prompt. - Closure path: parent observes task and possible shared-file collision → model assigns disjoint edits/worktrees to children → independent children act → parent integrates outcomes and feeds corrective tasks to specific children. - Boundary reachability: selecting shipped Flagship mode registers child tools inside an ordinary Sailry Host conversation; no custom downstream product is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordination choice (who edits which conflicting slice and how to reconcile) is the lead model&#39;s, while the queue itself is a fixed controller, not the basis of A. - Distinct S1 units: separately admitted model-backed child sessions with separate roles, tasks, status and results. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent writes to overlapping source files/worktrees and competing verification could invalidate the project build or another child&#39;s changes. - Attenuating coordination relation: parent-created disjoint ownership/worktree assignment, parent retention of shared files, and one consolidated verification/integration wave. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child outputs and test failures are gathered by the parent, which can send guideagent to the owning child or assign a next wave. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the shipped policy names a concrete inter-agent write collision and gives the parent a model-selected isolation and correction policy, beyond merely launching several generic workers. - Evidence: Flagship ownership and correction instructions, spawn/guide operational contract, child control. - Basis: explicit coordination policy with structural child execution and feedback. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: disjoint-file discipline is model-instruction based and not a hard source-file lock; default Standard/Plan sessions do not instantiate several S1 units.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain current task-wave coherence across the Flagship team&#39;s child commitments and integrated test outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child work unfinished or failed, mismatched implementation slices, repeated expensive verification and combined build failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: lead model observes child completion/result, directs failed work to its owning child, keeps shared/integration edits itself and decides whether the combined task is acceptable. - Decision owner: Flagship lead model in the same first-party parent session, at the task-team recursion level; Node&#39;s child state serves as feedback, not the decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: returned durable child statuses/responses, waitagent/guideagent, explicit wave-level integration instructions and consolidated builds/tests. - Closure path: admitted child status and final result → parent views work across the current team → model redirects/reconciles assignments → agent child work or shared-file edits → consolidated verification and final decision. - Boundary reachability: Flagship and delegation control are compiled into supported Host agent operation; child roles and budget are captured in the parent context. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the lead model exercises team-wide present-task control and error correction; routine scheduling/queue accounting alone is not credited as S3. - Whole-system current view: the parent receives the roster, concurrency budget, individual child status/results and consolidated project checks across the bounded Flagship task team. - Current-control decision scope: the parent can retain or change shared integration steps, guide/reassign follow-up work to child sessions, and accept/reject the assembled result. - Evidence: Flagship parent oversight instructions, delegation context and guide/wait, child result/status feedback. - Basis: explicit task-team control policy and runtime status feedback. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: scoped to one Flagship team&#39;s current workload, not product-wide host governance or autonomous organizational resource allocation; source review does not prove the model always follows the prompt.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated separate complementary audit right with independent evidence access and corrective return to S3. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect code or unsupported child claims may be found in optional review/testing, but these are not an independently constituted operational audit channel. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Flagship parent can request a read-only reviewer or run tests; no separate obligatory auditor has independent access and corrective authority. - Decision owner: same parent model arranges and accepts review results; developer tests and CI are adjacent validation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role-matched read-only children, shared test suite, test/build output, approval UI. - Closure path: task review or consolidated test output → parent may correct/redelegate; this remains inside ordinary task supervision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a read-only delegated child does not alone prove an S3-specific independent witness and feedback path. - Evidence: review delegation and verification instructions, tool roles/permissions. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: independently staffed review may be configured in a particular installation but was not verified as shipped complementary-audit authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party future-environment intelligence loop that evaluates adaptive operating options and feeds a capability choice back into S3. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dynamic provider metadata and model scores help users select existing models, while browsing and research tools serve current tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user configures providers; task model can browse or search for immediate task purposes; no strategic adaptation authority is evidenced. - Decision owner: user for model settings and task agent for in-task research, neither a shown S4 prospective owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: models.dev/ModelDial catalog download, web tools, provider settings. - Closure path: catalog data can inform model selection and tool research can solve the current request; no prospect → option → changed whole-system capability feedback path is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: external information alone is not an S4 future-facing organizational intelligence function. - Evidence: README model metadata and capabilities, model settings. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: prospective work can be manually requested of an agent but does not establish a persistent first-party organizational S4 owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no evidenced ultimate-purpose/identity conflict adjudication and authoritative policy-return cycle. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user/project trust, tool permissions, role selection and approval constraints restrict actions in a task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user grants/denies permissions and can revoke project trust; developer fixes tool and mode limits, but neither is a dedicated system-identity decision pathway. - Decision owner: user/developer for ordinary configuration and consent; no separate ultimate policy owner operationally identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: captured permission scopes, trust gate, role constraints, explicit stop/cancellation and project instructions. - Closure path: permission denied/approved or trust revoked → current task action allowed/stopped; no identity-level conflict → authority → policy decision → returned organizational rule. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: strong authorization gates are not an S5 policy-identity deliberation mechanism. - Evidence: project trust lifecycle, mode authority separation, README approvals. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: human controls legitimate use, but the first-party product supplies no evidenced identity-governance loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4c727003c4612ae0afe467dc4ea28463b68238c9/assessments/sa/sailry.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4c727003c4612ae0afe467dc4ea28463b68238c9\">4c72700 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T16:01:52+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/paper-instruments/feather-train\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Feather</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/paper-instruments/feather-train/tree/cdb5f34bad11c0c75ca4124b25a3e5da86bb62e2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision cdb5f34bad11c0c75ca4124b25a3e5da86bb62e2\">cdb5f34 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute user knowledge work and child assignments into deliverables. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing file contents, incomplete sources, tool errors, model-response interruptions and task-specific analysis. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next tool/action and decide how returned evidence changes the work or final answer. - Decision owner: parent/child model agent within its assigned task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool schema validation, local file/shell/MCP transport, turn caps, retries, compaction and saved traces. - Closure path: model action → tool invocation → ToolMessage → next model decision → deliverable/final answer. - Boundary reachability: CLI run calls runlocal; the packaged local runner constructs FeatherAgent for parent and children. No developer-only dogfood actor is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves transport and caps but no task-specific discretionary next action. - Evidence: runner, local wiring. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source-backed availability, not an empirical claim of deliverable quality or long-run viability.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: prevent the operational team from overwriting shared deliverables or duplicating one another&#39;s work. - Distinct S1 units: parent and independently executing child assignments, including multiple concurrent children. - Inter-S1 disturbance: agents with separate histories/read revisions can edit the same shared file from incompatible prior versions; duplicated assignments can also consume effort on the same work. - Attenuating coordination relation: injected parent guidance assigns allowed files and one assembling writer for a shared workbook/document/deck; child guidance preserves others&#39; edits and prohibits writing files another agent is changing. The parent chooses these scopes and revises work through follow-ups/stop. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: statuses/reports enter the parent&#39;s next model turn; follow-up assignments specify revised work. File tools return stale-read rejection into the offending agent&#39;s next turn. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the shipped guidance specifically addresses concurrent same-file interference, chooses exclusive assembly responsibilities and prohibits overlapping writes; this is additional to generic spawn/wait. - Disturbance / variety regulated: shared-file ownership and stale views across distinct operational agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose who may write which deliverable and reconcile overlapping work before further changes. - Decision owner: the autonomous parent agent; children apply their scoped instructions locally. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: assignment/report tools, separate revision maps, stale-read checks. Guidance is not an atomic lock and shell writes can bypass file-tool checks. - Closure path: assignment with allowed paths → child action/report → parent reconciliation/follow-up → subsequent scoped work; stale file-tool edits return an error requiring current evidence. - Boundary reachability: runlocal appends PARENTGUIDANCE, supplies CHILDGUIDANCE through Subagents, and exposes team tools in the supported production mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses the actual partition and corrective instruction; deterministic revision checking only rejects a preselected invalid write. - Evidence: coordination guidance, parent wiring, file-tool revision feedback, scripted shared-file test. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a supported behavioral regulation path, not proof that every model follows it or that simultaneous shell writes are impossible. Single-agent mode has no inter-unit coordination demand.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current commitments of the task team and intervene when a child is unnecessary, incomplete or no longer useful to the whole deliverable. - Whole-system current view: parent context plus listagents statuses, selected waitagent reports, failed/exhausted partial-work diagnostics and compacted team-state reminders. - Current-control decision scope: decide active assignments, restrict scope/side effects, follow up on gaps, stop owned children and decide which completed work supports the overall result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete/failed children, conflicting findings and currently active commitments competing for bounded concurrency. - Decisive decision or feedback right: revise commitments and terminate a selected child after reviewing present team state; this goes beyond initial task decomposition. - Decision owner: autonomous parent model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configured max-concurrent/total/turn limits, status registry, cancellation and awaited resource cleanup. The host owns fixed ceilings; these are not themselves autonomous resource bargaining. - Closure path: current reports/status → model selects follow-up/stop/new work → manager changes child lifecycle → results/status feed the next parent decision. - Boundary reachability: standard production team exposes all five team tools to the parent and constructs children; local runner owns the manager lifetime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing parent discretion leaves fixed caps and automatic teardown, but no evidence-dependent choice of which commitment to revise or stop. - Evidence: model-facing team tools, status/follow-up, stop/cleanup, parent responsibility. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: one local task team&#39;s current control, not organization-wide portfolio control or authority to change host ceilings. The noninteractive CLI and general embedding API supply no distinct function-specific human-parent mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material independent complementary audit path in the assessed task-team distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: erroneous operational claims are handled by ordinary checks and parent result reconciliation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate audit judgment/feedback owner established. - Decision owner: ordinary producing agents and parent remain responsible for QA. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: execution traces, artifact reads and test tools provide observability. - Closure path: routine checking stays in production; no independent audit-to-control loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a child can be asked to check work, but the generic assignment API supplies no specifically independent auditor role or complementary audit contract. - Evidence: subagent responsibility and reporting, adjacent grading, external Harbor adapter. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a host can construct a different audited system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped external-and-prospective adaptation loop at the local team boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: web/source information and model/context issues are processed as current task work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no adaptation-options-to-current-capability decision path established. - Decision owner: host selects configuration; producing model handles tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: optional web search/fetch, skills, compaction and separate training recipe. - Closure path: source retrieval enters task context; no distinct prospective capability-regulation conversation is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic research or model training alone does not establish S4. - Evidence: supported runtime composition, Train&#39;s external recipe, package separation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: future custom research assignments are not bounded absence counterexamples.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy resolution loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task scope, source trust and host constraints are applied as preselected guidance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity-level case → legitimate ultimate authority → returned policy path supplied. - Decision owner: host/user sets task and instructions; ordinary parent model controls current task work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system guidance, profile validation, filesystem boundaries and configured limits. - Closure path: constraints govern tools; no distinct ultimate-policy decision-return loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: being the root task agent or accepting host instructions does not grant ultimate-policy authority. - Evidence: production guidance, CLI config/run, supported embedding limits. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an operator&#39;s ability to replace instructions is not itself function-specific parent governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/48be0b607dc2399a7f268e985f4645b26679a84a/assessments/fe/feather.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 48be0b607dc2399a7f268e985f4645b26679a84a\">48be0b6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T03:07:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/whoops-1/termagent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TermAgent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-30\">2026-09-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/whoops-1/termagent/tree/33aa966551fcc2c050c0a213642d9966c3dd4a21\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 33aa966551fcc2c050c0a213642d9966c3dd4a21\">33aa966 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform coding work by interpreting the current objective, selecting permitted repository/tool actions, executing them and revising subsequent actions from returned evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: source/worktree state, implementation alternatives, provider uncertainty, tool/build/test failures, context pressure, no-progress/repeated actions and changing task evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next task-specific tool/action and revise it from tool/provider/workspace feedback. - Decision owner: the model-backed TermAgent actor in each foreground or background Agent runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry; provider abstraction/routing; sessions; context budgeting/compaction; permissions; profiles; execution workflow; retry/no-progress guards; repository map; skills/plugins/MCP. - Closure path: task/current context → model decision → first-party tool execution → result persisted/returned → same actor selects another action or final response. - Boundary reachability: the shipped CLI, headless/server surfaces and detached workers instantiate the same first-party Agent/tool runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor leaves workflow/permission/session machinery but removes the open-ended choice of what repository action to perform next; deterministic mechanisms constrain and transport rather than replace the coding decision. - Evidence: src/agent/agent.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: particular non-read actions can be parent-gated in ask, but TermAgent also ships auto as a first-party autonomous permission mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate destructive interference among multiple simultaneously active subordinate coding S1s sharing one project by bounding concurrency and enforcing non-overlapping writable workspace scopes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent background coding agents could edit the same or nested paths, overwrite one another, create non-reviewable shared-state races or exceed the bounded worker capacity for one session. - Distinct S1 units: each parallelagents task becomes a separately persisted agent task with its own detached worker process, provider configuration, prompt, status/output and declared scopePaths. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous agents share the project filesystem; overlapping file/directory scopes create a concrete mutation conflict risk. The design documentation explicitly names concurrent agents editing the same files as the conflict being prevented. - Attenuating coordination relation: the primary actor chooses 2–4 tasks and declares paths for each; parallelagents normalizes those paths, rejects out-of-project scopes, rejects pairwise overlaps, checks overlaps with already active agent scopes, enforces a maximum of four active workers and repeats the admission check under a cross-process session lock. Scoped workers are prevented from writing outside their declared region. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: accepted scopes constrain child write behavior; rejected overlap/capacity calls return errors to the primary model, which can redesign task decomposition/scopes or run work sequentially. Active scoped commitments are persisted and considered by later admissions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanism is expressly conditional on a concrete inter-S1 interference class—overlapping concurrent workspace mutation—and alters admission/write behavior specifically to attenuate it. Mere multiplicity or task routing is not the basis of the positive state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select which child S1 tasks may be launched together and assign their non-overlapping workspace ownership scopes; after scheduler feedback, revise those task/scope choices. - Decision owner: primary model-backed TermAgent actor in approvals=&#39;auto&#39;. Interactive ask can parent-gate launch authorization, but Methodology 0.3.x does not encode parent-mode notation for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parallelagents; scope normalization/overlap checks; activeAgentTasks; per-session capacity; cross-process session lock; scoped worker write checks; durable task records. - Closure path: primary actor decomposes work and proposes tasks/scopes → scheduler compares requested/current active scopes → launch is admitted or rejected → accepted children operate inside constrained scopes / rejection returns conflict evidence → subsequent primary decomposition can change. - Boundary reachability: parallelagents is registered in the standard CLI/server runtime together with first-party worker/task machinery; no application-authored coordinator is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code enforces safety once task/scopes are proposed, but without the primary model there is no task-specific choice of which independent coding commitments to create together or how to partition their work. The supported auto mode closes that discretion without parent approval. - Evidence: src/tools/parallel.ts; src/tasks/manager.ts; docs/PHASE3EXECUTION.md; src/tools/permissions.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: S2 is deliberately bounded to declared workspace-scope interference; it does not imply generic semantic coordination among agents whose declared scopes are disjoint. Human-gated ask runs remain supported, but S2 has no (P) publication state in Methodology 0.3.x.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate the current population of durable subordinate coding commitments by deciding what background work to start, observing its current status/output and terminating commitments that should no longer continue. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing current workload decomposition, independently progressing subordinate tasks, failed/stuck/unneeded workers, bounded concurrent capacity and the need to stop or redirect current commitments while foreground work continues. - Whole-system current view: each launch returns the durable IDs of the subordinate commitments that form the current bounded worker population; taskstatus exposes a selected task&#39;s durable status, PID, output, session identity, provider and scope state, while launch admission itself queries the complete active-agent set for the session before admitting more work. The foreground actor can therefore inspect the status/output of every commitment it created and receives scheduler feedback against the current active population. - Current-control decision scope: the primary actor can create independent durable coding commitments through backgroundagent or a bounded set through parallelagents, choose their prompts/scopes, and terminate queued/running commitments through model-facing taskcancel; these actions change the current subordinate workload population rather than merely the content of one S1 turn. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which new coding commitments should exist now, what scoped work they own, observe their current results/status, and cancel commitments after that feedback. - Decision owner: primary model-backed TermAgent actor in approvals=&#39;auto&#39;; interactive operator becomes decisive for shell-risk start/cancel authorization in approvals=&#39;ask&#39;. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: backgroundagent; parallelagents; taskstatus; taskcancel; TaskManager durable records/events; worker PID lifecycle/recovery; active-agent population query; capacity/session locks; permission gate. - Closure path: current task IDs/status/output and admission feedback → primary model decides start/parallel/cancel action → permission path → task manager spawns or signals worker → durable task state changes → subsequent taskstatus/scheduler/model turns observe that changed current population. - Boundary reachability: all lifecycle tools and worker/task machinery are first-party standard runtime components registered on supported CLI/server surfaces; the child workers reconstruct TermAgent&#39;s own provider/runtime state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in auto, removing the primary model leaves lifecycle machinery and fixed limits but no task-specific judgment about which commitment to create/cancel or how to decompose current work. In ask, the parent operator owns final authorization for the same commitment action. - Evidence: src/tools/background.ts; src/tools/parallel.ts; src/tasks/manager.ts; src/tools/permissions.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: there is no model-facing one-call tasklist; the positive current view is bounded to the commitment IDs returned to the parent plus per-task taskstatus and scheduler feedback over the complete active session set. This is sufficient for the small S3 claim but should not be read as a rich organization-wide dashboard.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material boundary-reachable complementary audit role with sufficiently independent access, audit judgment and corrective return was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary implementation defects and failed verification are regulated inside the production workflow, but no separate S3-specific audit disturbance/claim channel was established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for a complementary auditor. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic verifyproject; separate verification provider routing; workflow verification phase; tests/build commands; task status and ordinary repository inspection. - Closure path: absent at S3 level. The enforced planning/building/verifying/iterating controller routes ordinary production evidence back to the same coding workflow; no materially independent claim-access path and audit actor is required for that closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a role-specific provider does not by itself create an independent auditor, and deterministic verification results are ordinary operational QA. No separate actor was found whose audit judgment challenges the production actor through complementary evidence and then returns to corrective control. - Evidence: src/agent/workflow.ts; src/agent/agent.ts; docs/PHASE3EXECUTION.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an application could potentially create an audit-specific background agent with custom instructions, but uninstantiated composition is not credited as first-party closed S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop over TermAgent&#39;s own present organizational capability was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository-map retrieval, external/provider switching, skills/plugins/MCP, sessions and role-specific routing can improve current execution but do not close an outside-and-then capability-adaptation function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider/router profiles; fallback providers; repository map; skills/plugins; MCP; project instructions; session/context compaction. - Closure path: no environmental/future distinction → adaptation-option generation/evaluation → adopted change to present organizational capability/S3 loop was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current provider routing and retrieval choose among preconfigured capabilities for the task; they do not let a first-party S4 actor prospectively redesign/adopt organizational capabilities from external/future evidence. - Evidence: README.md; src/agent/agent.ts; docs/PHASE3EXECUTION.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: developer/operator addition of providers/plugins/skills changes future capability but is adjacent configuration work unless the running organization itself closes that prospective adaptation decision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure was established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: approval modes, profiles, tool risks, scoped-worker restrictions, project instructions and configuration constrain current operation but do not constitute an ultimate-policy/identity decision loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5-level identity or ultimate policy. - Decision owner: not established at S5; developer/operator configuration sets the operative envelope outside a qualifying runtime identity closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionGate; approval config; build/plan/explore profiles; workflow phase rules; scoped worker prohibitions; project instructions; provider/plugin/MCP configuration. - Closure path: absent at S5 level; no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned policy governing subsequent operation was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: models act within configured policy but do not own the legitimate right to redefine TermAgent&#39;s identity or ultimate rules. Operator approval of a launch/tool call is ordinary parent governance at that function, not S5. - Evidence: src/tools/permissions.ts; src/config/config.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: project/repository governance determines future releases but remains adjacent to the runtime boundary and is not imported as product S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/80571eb4795983f28168746a9ce20d3a4df460c6/assessments/te/termagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 80571eb4795983f28168746a9ce20d3a4df460c6\">80571eb &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T17:42:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gvergnaud/bise\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bise</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gvergnaud/bise/tree/2a0cf0caf75cb17e9b6ce40e90bab517e963e425\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2a0cf0caf75cb17e9b6ce40e90bab517e963e425\">2a0cf0c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous task execution - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain code state, tool results and user requests - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose next tool/action and task completion report - Decision owner: Provider-backed main/task agent - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bend tool dispatch, sessions, compaction, bash/edit/MCP and report persistence - Closure path: Tool results re-enter the agent loop; changed files and terminal report are returned to main/user - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Provider-backed main/task agent; A is architectural availability, not measured success; no provider was invoked. - Evidence: bend/runtime/main.bend; bend/runtime/repl-live.bend; rust/switchboard/src/prompts.rs lines 115–206 - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: A is architectural availability, not measured success; no provider was invoked. - Boundary reachability: Bend provider/tool loop and durable hub; Rust switchboard role construction, devflow, task/control commands, inbox and worktree machinery wired into shipped terminal/desktop clients. The cited decision path is shipped in this mode and returns through Bend tool dispatch, sessions, compaction, bash/edit/MCP and report persistence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Prevent destructive interference between concurrent task agents - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two task units editing the same files or observing half-finished changes - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose disjoint files, sequencing, shared place or isolated worktree based on the actual tasks - Decision owner: Main agent - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sb task board, spawn/place commands, worktree creation and landing machinery - Closure path: Main&#39;s placement decision determines the directories and integration paths used by subsequent worker actions - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Main agent; Git isolation alone is enforcement; the shipped main role supplies contextual coordination discretion. - Evidence: rust/switchboard/src/devflow.rs lines 393–425; rust/switchboard/src/prompts.rs lines 124–145 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Git isolation alone is enforcement; the shipped main role supplies contextual coordination discretion. - Boundary reachability: Bend provider/tool loop and durable hub; Rust switchboard role construction, devflow, task/control commands, inbox and worktree machinery wired into shipped terminal/desktop clients. The cited decision path is shipped in this mode and returns through sb task board, spawn/place commands, worktree creation and landing machinery. - Distinct S1 units: Concurrent task agents producing separate operational outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Two task units editing the same files or observing half-finished changes - Attenuating coordination relation: Choose disjoint files, sequencing, shared place or isolated worktree based on the actual tasks - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Main&#39;s placement decision determines the directories and integration paths used by subsequent worker actions - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Git isolation alone is enforcement; the shipped main role supplies contextual coordination discretion. Prevent destructive interference between concurrent task agents\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Current control over the project task team - Disturbance / variety regulated: Blocked commitments, changing constraints and capability allocation among live tasks - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect current team and revise briefs, constraints, agent model or intervention; alternatively user redirects team tasks - Decision owner: Main in base mode; legitimate local project operator in parent mode - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Injected bisestate/taskstatus, sb tasks/send/model, inbox and correction messages - Closure path: Reports/questions reach main; main&#39;s revised instruction or model applies to subsequent task turns; user decisions are returned by addressed correction/answer - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Main in base mode; legitimate local project operator in parent mode; Not inferred from the word orchestrator or from spawning alone. Parent current intervention is confined to this one project hub. - Evidence: rust/switchboard/src/prompts.rs lines 124–154; rust/switchboard/src/devflow.rs lines 402–445; README.md control/talk-to-any-agent - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Not inferred from the word orchestrator or from spawning alone. Parent current intervention is confined to this one project hub. - Boundary reachability: Bend provider/tool loop and durable hub; Rust switchboard role construction, devflow, task/control commands, inbox and worktree machinery wired into shipped terminal/desktop clients. The cited decision path is shipped in this mode and returns through Injected bisestate/taskstatus, sb tasks/send/model, inbox and correction messages. - Whole-system current view: The cited current state/report path covers all units in the declared deployment. - Current-control decision scope: Inspect current team and revise briefs, constraints, agent model or intervention; alternatively user redirects team tasks\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No separate complementary audit supplied for this boundary - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unreliable worker completion claims - Decisive decision or feedback right: No independently owned corrective-audit right established - Decision owner: None established - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task reports, ordinary tests, history and optional generic review tasks - Closure path: Main is explicitly told to accept done reports and not re-run their tests; failed/blocked/no-check reports receive ordinary control - Why this is / is not agent-owned: None established; Development proofs and routine worker QA do not establish independent operational audit. - Evidence: rust/switchboard/src/prompts.rs lines 126–127 and 198–205; bend/laws/runtime.bend - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped external-and-prospective adaptation loop - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing future tool/provider/environment suitability - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party prospective option-to-capability decision loop established - Decision owner: None established - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plugin installation, per-agent model selection, schedules, compaction and self.reload - Closure path: These support present operation or software updates; no supplied actor couples environmental futures to changed current capability - Why this is / is not agent-owned: None established; An arbitrary research task could be commissioned, but generic task/tool expressiveness is not an S4 constructor. - Evidence: bend/runtime/selftools-pure.bend; rust/switchboard/src/prompts.rs; README.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop supplied - Disturbance / variety regulated: System identity or ultimate policy in dispute - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific ultimate-authority decision/return protocol established - Decision owner: Operator sets ordinary configuration; no qualifying S5 owner established - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role prompts, permissions, inbox approvals, saved PR/trunk flow - Closure path: Flow choice and notification responses govern operational integration; they do not themselves establish identity-level or ultimate-policy deliberation - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Operator sets ordinary configuration; no qualifying S5 owner established; A real human decision path was considered; its matters are ordinary task/shipping controls, not automatic S5. - Evidence: rust/switchboard/src/devflow.rs lines 402–445; rust/switchboard/src/prompts.rs lines 150–156; prompts/prompt-bise.txt - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ac6d6474ac3a34f33e646cd534adb2f22017c1d9/assessments/bi/bise.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ac6d6474ac3a34f33e646cd534adb2f22017c1d9\">ac6d647 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T19:57:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Oklahomawhore/AgentHarness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Oklahomawhore/AgentHarness/tree/6bedcf45fad75e21df5e5cac827a898ef5c7d228\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6bedcf45fad75e21df5e5cac827a898ef5c7d228\">6bedcf4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user-directed engineering work through repeated model-selected tool actions and feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user objectives, workspace/process state, tool outcomes and failures, model context pressure, follow-up input, child outcomes and external tool/provider responses. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive tool/action and revise the next step from returned evidence, or conclude the turn. - Decision owner: the active Agent model in the root Session; child models own the same local operational discretion within delegated child Sessions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent driver, Session event log, tool registry/execution scheduler, sandbox and approval services, LLM adapters, compaction/retry hooks and persistence. - Closure path: admitted user/session context → model request → assistant tool call/action → runtime execution → ordered tool/result / durable event → next model step observes the result and chooses a revised action or final response. - Boundary reachability: the Agent loop and core tools are mounted by the standard base-backed TUI/Web/headless product compositions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model removes the task-specific choice of tool, arguments, sequencing and stopping point; deterministic runtime machinery validates, schedules and executes those choices but does not make materially equivalent operational judgments. - Evidence: docs/agent-lifecycle.md; packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among concurrently operating child Agents sharing the same workspace by deciding which delegated work is sufficiently independent to overlap and by revising/steering work when current child state requires coordination. - Disturbance / variety regulated: sibling child Sessions can run concurrently against one shared workspace or external resources and can race or invalidate one another when their effects overlap. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task decomposition and whether multiple child commitments are independent enough to launch together; subsequently message or interrupt continuable children as coordination needs change. - Decision owner: the parent Agent model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parallel-safe delegation execution, separate child Sessions/identities, ordered parent result commit, durable continuable child identities, sendmessage, interruptagent, settlement notices and maxParallelToolCalls enforcement. - Closure path: parent model identifies independent work → emits multiple subagent delegations together (or keeps dependent work sequential) → children operate concurrently in separate Sessions against the shared environment → settlement/status/message evidence returns to parent context → parent may steer/interrupt/redelegate or change its own subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: the base bundle mounts the continuable subagent tool and its fixed model-visible guidance to &quot;Start independent delegations together&quot;, along with the follow-up control surface; the shared-workspace race is an implemented/shipped behavior rather than test-only topology. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the scheduler treats delegation as concurrency-safe and cannot infer whether free-text child tasks have disjoint workspace effects; the project explicitly leaves sibling workspace coordination to the model. Removing that model judgment leaves only unconditional overlap/caps, not materially equivalent task-specific coordination. - Evidence: packages/subagent/tool-subagent/README.md; packages/subagent/tool-subagent-control/README.md; packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml; .agents/notes/archived/feature/2026-08-09-parallel-subagent-delegations.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: the runtime does not provide a write-lease conflict solver; S2 credit is for the model-owned independence/timing/steering relation, not for the generic mailbox or parallel scheduler. - Distinct S1 units: root Agent plus two or more separately persisted child-agent Sessions, each with its own model/tool operation and durable identity. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent sibling children can race on shared workspace files or external resources and thereby invalidate or interfere with peer work. - Attenuating coordination relation: parent-model decomposition/independence judgment determines what is co-scheduled; continuable children can then receive model-selected follow-up steering or interruption. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child settlement notices, listagents status and accepted messages/interrupts feed current child outcomes into later parent decisions; follow-up input changes child execution at a later step boundary. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited relation is tied to the repository&#39;s explicit shared-workspace sibling-race disturbance and changes which child commitments overlap or are subsequently steered; Task/Room messaging alone is not credited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain current control over the root Agent&#39;s active continuable child organization: create commitments, observe the current descendant portfolio and intervene in live child work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing child lifecycle state, completed/failed/idle/running commitments, new information requiring follow-up, runaway or no-longer-useful child work, and current need to change delegated effort. - Decisive decision or feedback right: create children with task/persona/tool/model/depth commitments, inspect the continuable descendant tree, send task-changing follow-up input and interrupt a child&#39;s current turn. - Decision owner: the parent Agent model for its current child organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable child catalog and Session identities, continuation manager, live Agent registry, authorization/lineage checks, listagents, sendmessage, interruptagent, settlement notices and depth/capacity enforcement. - Closure path: parent creates current child commitments → listagents/settlement notices expose current descendant status → parent chooses follow-up, interruption, additional delegation or its own integration action → runtime delivers/enforces the choice → subsequent child/root operation changes. - Boundary reachability: the shipped base bundle mounts continuable spawn children and all three model-facing control tools; no custom downstream controller is required for this mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: lifecycle and authorization services enforce identity and interruption, but they do not decide which child should be started, redirected or stopped for the current objective. Those task-specific current-control decisions belong to the parent model. - Evidence: packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml; packages/subagent/tool-subagent/README.md; packages/subagent/tool-subagent-control/README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: listagents intentionally excludes one-shot children. S3 is established by the supported continuable-child mode, where the root can enumerate the complete continuable descendant tree; this does not claim equivalent supervision over every optional child mode. - Whole-system current view: for the continuable-child mode, listagents(scope=descendants) walks the root&#39;s complete continuable child tree and reports durable identity, direct parent/depth and current running, idle or ready status; settlement notices add outcome/final-message feedback. - Current-control decision scope: create/withhold child commitments; select task, route/persona/tool/depth settings where supported; send new work/steering to direct continuable children; interrupt current child turns; launch further work or integrate returned outcomes.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material shipped complementary-audit function was established that independently challenges operational reporting or completion through a distinct access path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential inaccurate child claims or hidden operational failure were considered, but the standard distribution does not close an independent audit loop for them. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. - Decision owner: none established at the assessed boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session/event durability, task status, approval logs, ordinary child transcripts/status and optional development evidence infrastructure provide observability or validation but do not form an independent complementary audit function. - Closure path: no qualifying independent audit finding → organizational response closure was found in the shipped mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary parent result/status observation is the in-band S3 path; no separate autonomous auditor or shipped audit judgment path owns a complementary verdict. - Evidence: packages/subagent/tool-subagent-control/README.md; docs/subsystems/development-room.md; .agents/notes/archived/feature/2026-08-02-session-search-not-shipped-default.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective organizational adaptation loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future environmental/capability change can be researched through ordinary tools and shared Task context, but no shipped mechanism converts such intelligence into a governed change of the harness&#39;s present capability/organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S4. - Decision owner: none established at the assessed boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MCP/tool discovery, development-evidence providers, skills, settings, Task publications, model-route selection and compaction alter current task information/configuration but do not close a prospective adaptation function. - Closure path: no qualifying external/prospective option → capability-change → subsequent-operation loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-task research and configuration choices remain S1/support behavior; they do not grant an actor organizational adaptation authority. - Evidence: packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml; docs/subsystems/development-room.md; repository collaboration/evidence package surfaces. - Basis: structural absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material runtime identity / ultimate-policy closure is established inside the assessed harness organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: sandbox, approvals, system instructions, model/tool settings and profile composition constrain operation but do not constitute an S5 identity/policy issue handled by ultimate organizational authority and returned into operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5 at this recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system prompt assembly, sandbox policy, user approval, model/provider settings, profile/bundle configuration and Task context authority boundaries. - Closure path: no qualifying identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authority → authoritative policy decision → returned operational change loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main model acts under configured identity and permission constraints but does not own the ultimate right to redefine the organization&#39;s identity or governing policy. - Evidence: packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml; docs/agent-lifecycle.md; approval and collaboration boundaries described in repository documentation. - Basis: explicit + structural absence - Confidence: high - Caveats: ordinary user approvals and configuration edits constrain actions but Methodology 0.3.6 does not promote generic approval/configuration to S5 or parent-governed S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/41869737c521e1cc01cf533dc57e55ada3450f2c/assessments/agentharness-oklahomawhore.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 41869737c521e1cc01cf533dc57e55ada3450f2c\">4186973 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
